window.__GNAMMA_SANANDAJI84.push(...[{"id":"I132.lower.02","volume":1,"page":132,"physical_pdf_page":145,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله فلو اتحد المحل الخ) والجواب بأن عدم الامتياز لا يدل على الاتحاد وارتفاع الاثنينية بل على عدم العلم بالاثنينية ليس بشيء لأن ما ذكر من الاستدلال على تقدير تمامه يفيد عدم الامتياز في نفس الأمر لا عند العقل فقط","english":"On “If the subject too were one”: the answer that absence of distinction establishes only our ignorance of twoness, not identity and the abolition of twoness, is worthless. If the proposed proof succeeds, it establishes absence of distinction in reality, not merely in the intellect.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I132.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I132.lower.03","volume":1,"page":132,"physical_pdf_page":145,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله مستندة إلى أسباب مفارقة الخ) قال في شرح المقاصد وبهذا يندفع ما ذكر في الدليل من أن نسبة العوارض إلى كل منهما على السوية فلا تعرض لأحدهما خاصة بل لكل منهما وحينئذ لا يبقى الامتياز انتهى ثم قد يستدل على امتناع اجتماع المثلين بأنه لو جاز لجاز لمن له علم نظري بشيء أن ينظر لتحصيل العلم به إذ لا مانع سوى امتناع اجتماع المثلين وقد يجاب بمنع الملازمة لجواز مانع آخر كأنتفاء شرط النظر وهو عدم العلم بالمطلوب فتأمل وتمسكت المعتزلة في وقوع الاجتماع بأن الجسم يعرض له سواد ثم آخر وهكذا إلى أن يبلغ غايته وأجيب بأن السوادات المتفاوتة في الشدة والضعف مختلفة فيعرض في كل آن للجسم الذي يشتد سواده نوع آخر (قوله والعدمين الخ)","english":"On “Dependent on separate causes”: Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid says that this removes the proof’s claim that contingent features stand in the same relation to both accidents and therefore cannot attach specifically to one rather than both, leaving no distinction. End. Another argument against the coexistence of similars is that, were it possible, someone already possessing inferential knowledge of something could undertake reasoning to acquire knowledge of it again, since only the impossibility of coexistence would prevent this. The consequence may be denied: another impediment is possible, such as the absence of a condition of reasoning—ignorance of what is sought. Reflect. The Muʿtazila argue for actual coexistence from a body’s acquiring blackness, then another blackness, and so on until it reaches the maximum. The answer is that blacknesses differing in intensity are different: at each instant a body whose blackness intensifies acquires a different species. On “Two absences”—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I132.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I133.lower.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I132.upper.01","volume":1,"page":132,"physical_pdf_page":145,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"بالمحل فلو اتحد المحل أيضا ارتفعت الاثنينية فلا تماثل لارتفاع الاثنينية لكنه قال في شرح المقاصد ورد هذا بالمنع لجواز أن يتخصص كل بعوارض مستندة إلى أسباب مفارقة (و) منها (التضاد وهو كون المعنيين بحيث يستحيل لذاتيهما اجتماعهما في محل واحد من جهة واحدة) المراد بالمعنى ما يقابل العين أي ما لا يكون قائما بنفسه واحترز به عن العينين والعين مع المعنى والعدمين والعدم مع الوجود وظاهر التعريف يتناول القديم والحادث كالعلمين كعلم الله وعلم زيد إذا لا إشعار فيه باشتراط التوارد على محل واحد وقد يقال إن","english":"—is through its subject. If the subject too were one, twoness would disappear, and with it similarity. Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid, however, rejects this by denying the premise: each accident may be individuated through contingent features dependent on separate causes. Another general attribute is “contrariety: two attributes being such that their natures make their coexistence in one subject in one respect impossible.” “Attribute” here contrasts with a concrete entity: it is what does not subsist in itself. This excludes two concrete entities, an entity and an attribute, two absences, and an absence with an existent. The apparent definition includes an eternal and an originated attribute, such as God’s knowledge and Zayd’s knowledge, because it does not express the requirement that the two can successively occupy one subject. It may be said that—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I132.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I131.upper.01","join_out":"I133.upper.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I133.lower.01","volume":1,"page":133,"physical_pdf_page":146,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"ولذا قالوا بعدم التضاد في الأحكام وسائر الأمور الإضافية لكونها أمورا اعتبارية لا تحقق لها في الأعيان (قوله لأن تصور حركة الشيء الخ) ولذا يصح الحكم باستحالتهما","english":"For this reason they deny contrariety in judgments and other relative matters, since these are matters of consideration without external realisation. On “Since conceiving the thing’s motion, etc.”: this is why it is legitimate to judge their [simultaneous occurrence] impossible.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I133.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I132.lower.final","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I133.lower.02","volume":1,"page":133,"physical_pdf_page":146,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله احتراز عن خروج مثل الصغر الخ) ولقد أحسن الشارح «مد ظله» بزيادة لفظ الخروج ليسلم مما أورد على العضد حيث قال واحترز بقيد جهة واحدة عن مثل الصغر والكبر على الإطلاق فإنهما لا يتضادان وإن امتنع اجتماعهما في الجملة أي وقت اعتبار إضافتهما إلى معين واعترض عليه في شرح المقاصد بأنه لا خفاء في أنه لا حاجة إلى هذا القيد لأن مطلق الصغر والكبر لا يمتنع اجتماعهما وعند اتحاد الجهة يمتنع ذلك فالأقرب أن القيد احتراز عن خروج مثل ذلك انتهى أقول لعل البعض أراد أن مثل ذلك إذا اعتبر من حيث الإطلاق كان خارجا من التضاد وإذا اعتبر من حيث الإضافة إلى معين كان داخلا فيه ومتجها لكن الأقرب هو أن مثل ذلك معدود من أضداد لصدق التعريف عليه ولعل لفظ الأقرب إشارة","english":"On “Prevents the exclusion of smallness, etc.” The commentator—may his protection endure—has done well to add “exclusion,” thereby avoiding the objection made against al-ʿAḍud. Al-ʿAḍud said that “in one respect” excludes unrestricted smallness and largeness: they are not contraries, even though their coexistence may be impossible when both are referred to a determinate thing. Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid objects that the qualification is evidently unnecessary on that account: smallness and largeness without restriction can coexist, whereas with the respect held constant they cannot. More plausibly, the qualification prevents examples of this kind from being excluded. End of quotation. I say: perhaps the earlier writer meant that, considered without restriction, they fall outside contrariety, whereas considered relative to a determinate thing they fall within it; that is defensible. But it is more plausible to count them among contraries because the definition applies to them. Perhaps the expression “more plausibly” indicates—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I133.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I134.lower.01","readings":[{"locus":"من أضداد / البعض","status":"These short expressions are provisionally retained; the explicit distinction between exclusion and prevention of exclusion is secure."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I133.upper.01","volume":1,"page":133,"physical_pdf_page":146,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"معنى امتناع الاجتماع أنهما يتواردان على محل ولا يكونان معا فيخرج مثل ذلك لأن محل القديم قديم فلا يتصف بالحادث وبالعكس ولأن القديم لا يزول عن المحل حتى يرد عليه المقابل وقوله لذاتيهما احتراز عن مثل العلم بحركة الشيء والعلم بسكونه في آن واحد بمعنى العلم بأنه متحرك والعلم بأنه ساكن فإنهما لا يجتمعان لكن لا لذاتيهما بل لامتناع اجتماع الحركة والسكون المعلومين وإنما قلنا بمعنى العلم بأنه متحرك والعلم بأنه ساكن لأن تصور حركة الشيء وسكونه ممكن وقوله بحيث يستحيل اجتماعهما في محل واحد احتراز عن مثل السواد والحلاوة يمكن اجتماعهما في محل واحد وقوله من جهة واحدة احتراز عن خروج مثل الصغر والكبر وكذا القرب والبعد فإنه لا يمتنع اجتماعهما عند اختلاف الجهة ويمتنع عند اتحاد الجهة كما إذا اعتبر إضافتهما إلى معين ككون واحد صغيرا وكبيرا بالنسبة إلى زيد وربما يعترض على تعريف المتضادين","english":"—the impossibility of coexistence means that the two can successively occupy a subject but cannot be present together. Such an example is excluded: the subject of an eternal attribute is eternal and cannot be qualified by an originated attribute, and conversely; moreover, the eternal attribute never leaves its subject to allow its opposite to occupy it. “In themselves” excludes such things as knowing that something moves and knowing that it is at rest at the same instant. These cannot coexist, not because of their own natures, but because the motion and rest known cannot coexist. We specified knowing “that it moves” and “that it is at rest,” since merely conceiving its motion and rest is possible. “Such that their coexistence in a single subject is impossible” excludes blackness and sweetness, which can coexist in one subject. “In one respect” prevents the exclusion of smallness and largeness, and likewise nearness and remoteness: these may coexist when the respect differs, but not when it is the same—for example, when both are referred to a determinate thing, so that one thing would be both small and large relative to Zayd. An objection is sometimes raised against the definition of contraries—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I133.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I132.upper.final","join_out":"I134.upper.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I134.lower.01","volume":1,"page":134,"physical_pdf_page":147,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"إلى ذلك فتأمله فإنه دقيق (قوله ويجاب بأن اتحاد المحل شرط الخ) هذا الجواب مذكور مع اعتراضه في شرح المقاصد ولعله أراد به ما مر من أنه قد يقال إن معنى امتناع الاجتماع أنهما يتواردان على محل ولا يكونان معا وأقول إنما يفيد إذا كان القائل بامتناع اجتماع المثلين ممن يقول بأن معنى امتناع اجتماع المتضادين هو أنهما يتواردان على محل ولا يكونان معا وأما إذا كان قائلا بأن معناه هو امتناع اجتماعهما مطلقا على ما سبق من ظاهر التعريف فلا وحينئذ الصواب هو أن يقال معنى امتناع اجتماع المتضادين أنهما يمتنع اجتماعهما في محل واحد مع بقاء الاثنينية فيهما بفرض الاجتماع فيه بخلاف المثلين عند ذلك القائل فإن معنى امتناع اجتماعهما فيه عنده هو أنه يرتفع الاثنينية منهما بفرض ذلك الاجتماع فتدبره جدا","english":"—this distinction. Consider it: it is subtle. On “The answer is that unity of subject is a condition”: this answer, together with an objection to it, occurs in Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid. Perhaps he intends the earlier suggestion that inability to coexist means that two things can successively occupy a subject but cannot be present together. I say: that helps only if the person denying the coexistence of similar things also understands the incompatibility of contraries in that way. If he instead means absolute inability to coexist, as the apparent definition suggests, it does not help. The correct distinction is that contraries cannot coexist in one subject while retaining their twoness on the supposition of coexistence. For the person in question, similar things cannot coexist because supposing them together abolishes their twoness. Consider this very carefully.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I134.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I133.lower.02","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I134.lower.02","volume":1,"page":134,"physical_pdf_page":147,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله فبالحقيقة أو بالعارض الخ) إن قيل إن اعتبار الغيرية بهذه الاعتبارات الثلاثة ينافي اعتبارها فيما سبق بحسب الذات والمفهوم فقط قلت هذا تقسيم باعتبار آخر فلا منافاة فتفطن","english":"On “Real, or accidental”: if it is objected that treating otherness under these three considerations conflicts with its previous consideration only in respect of essence and concept, I reply that this is a division under a different consideration. There is no conflict. Attend carefully.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I134.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I134.lower.03","volume":1,"page":134,"physical_pdf_page":147,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله فخرج بالمخالف المثلان الخ) إذا قيل إن سلم أن المثلين غير متخالفين كان الأولى فيما سبق أن يعتبر لخروجهما من تعريف المتضادين قيد التخالف أيضا (قلت) التخالف عند السابقين هو نفس التعدد الذي يساوق الغيرية وهي ثابتة في المثلين أيضا ضرورة كما سبق بخلافه على هذا الاصطلاح فإنه أخص من التعدد فيقابل المماثلة","english":"On “The requirement of difference excludes similar things”: if it is said that, once similar things are conceded not to be different, the earlier definition of contraries should likewise have used difference to exclude them, I reply that, for the earlier group, difference is simply plurality, coextensive with otherness. This necessarily applies to similar things too, as already stated. In the present terminology, however, difference is narrower than plurality and is opposed to similarity—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I134.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I135.lower.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I134.upper.01","volume":1,"page":134,"physical_pdf_page":147,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"بالمتماثلين كالسوادين عند من يقول بامتناع اجتماعهما ويجاب بأن اتحاد المحل شرط في التضاد ولا تماثل إلا عند اختلاف المحل ثم ما ذكر من معنى الغيرية والتماثل والتضاد على اصطلاح المتكلمين (وعند الفلاسفة كل اثنين غيران) فإن كانت الاثنينية بالحقيقة فبالحقيقة أو بالعارض فبالعارض أو بالاعتبار فبالاعتبار (فإن اشتركا في تمام الماهية) كزيد وعمرو في الإنسانية (فمثلان وإلا فمختلفان) سواء اشتركا في ذاتي أو عرضي أو لم يشتركا أصلا (وهما) أي المختلفان (متقابلان إن امتنع اجتماعهما في محل واحد من جهة واحدة) فخرج بالمخالف المثلان وإن امتنع اجتماعهما والتقييد بوحدة المحل لأن","english":"—from similar things, such as two blacknesses, according to those who deny that they can coexist. The answer is that unity of subject is a condition of contrariety, whereas similarity occurs only with distinct subjects. The preceding meanings of otherness, similarity and contrariety follow the theologians’ terminology. “For the philosophers, any two things are other.” If their twoness is real, their otherness is real; if accidental, accidental; if a matter of consideration, likewise. “If they share their entire quiddity,” as Zayd and ʿAmr share humanity, “they are similar; otherwise they are different,” whether they share something essential, something accidental, or nothing at all. “The two”—the different things—“are opposed if they cannot coexist in a single subject in one respect.” The requirement of difference excludes similar things, even if their coexistence is impossible. Unity of subject is stipulated because—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I134.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I133.upper.01","join_out":"I135.upper.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I135.lower.01","volume":1,"page":135,"physical_pdf_page":148,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"كما قال وإلا فمتخالفان فتدبر (قوله قد يجتمعان في الوجود) الناظر إلى هذا هو القيد بالمحل فقط فيلغو قيد الوحدة فالأولى أن يقول في الوجود وفي الجسم على الإطلاق كما ذكره في شرح المقاصد لينظر قيد المحل إلى الوجود والوحدة إلى الجسم المطلق أو ترك قيد الوحدة وإنما بعث الشارح «مد ظله» على ذلك تمثيل المصنف رحمه الله في شرح المقاصد لكلا الاجتماعين ببياض الرومي وسواد الحبشي فظن كفاية الاقتصار على أحدهما وليس كذلك فتأمله","english":"—as he says, “Otherwise they are different.” Reflect. On “They can coexist in existence”: only the qualification “subject” addresses this point, leaving “unity” redundant. It would be better to say “in existence and in body without restriction,” as Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid does, so that “subject” addresses existence and “unity” addresses body without restriction; alternatively, omit “unity.” What prompted the commentator was the author’s use, in Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid, of the Byzantine person’s whiteness and Abyssinian person’s blackness as an example of both kinds of coexistence. He therefore supposed that mentioning only one sufficed, but it does not. Consider this.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I135.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I134.lower.03","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I135.lower.02","volume":1,"page":135,"physical_pdf_page":148,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله وقد عرفت معنى امتناع اجتماعهما الخ) وهو أنهما يتواردان على محل ولا يكونان معا فيخرج الأعيان كالإنسان والفرس لعدم ورودهما على المحل أصلا وكذا يخرج القديم والحادث كعلم الله تعالى وعلم زيد لعدم تواردهما على محل واحد وعلى ذلك تعميم قوله فيخرج ما ليس كذلك فافهم وإنما لم يتعرض «مد ظله» فيما سبق لإخراج هذا القيد مثل الإنسان والفرس لخروجه بقيد المعنيين كما سبق لكن قال في شرح المقاصد ما حاصله أن فيه بحثا لأن الموضوع في التقابل والتضاد ليس بمعنى المحل المقوم حتى يلزم أن يكون المتقابلان من قبيل الأعراض البتة للقطع بتقابل الإيجاب والسلب في الجواهر مثل الفرسية واللافرسية بل صرح ابن سينا بالتضاد بين الصور واعتبار الورود على المحل الذي هو الهيولى انتهى (أقول) لو أريد بالمعنى في هذا الباب الصفة أعم من أن تكون من الصفات النفسية أو غيرها لالتأم به جوانب الكلام فتأمله","english":"On “You now know the meaning of their inability to coexist”: namely, that they successively occupy one subject but cannot be present together. This excludes concrete beings such as a human and a horse, since neither occupies a subject in that sense. It also excludes eternal and originated attributes, such as God’s knowledge and Zayd’s knowledge, since they cannot successively occupy one subject. Hence the general wording “what is not of this kind is excluded.” Understand this. The commentator did not earlier use this qualification to exclude human and horse because “two attributes” had already excluded them. Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid, however, questions this: “subject” in opposition and contrariety does not mean a sustaining substrate such that opposites must invariably be accidents. Affirmation and negation unquestionably oppose one another in substances, as equinity and nonequinity do. Avicenna even expressly affirms contrariety between forms, considering their occurrence in a subject that is prime matter. End of quotation. I say: if “attribute” here means a qualification broad enough to include essential qualifications and others, the different parts of the discussion fit together. Consider it.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I135.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I135.lower.03","volume":1,"page":135,"physical_pdf_page":148,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"ثم اعلم أنهم ذكروا أن من التقابل ما يجري في القضايا كالتناقض والتضاد فإن قولنا كل حيوان إنسان نقيض لقولنا بعض الحيوان ليس بإنسان وضد لقولنا لا شيء من الحيوان بإنسان مع أنه لا يتصور ورود القضايا على محل","english":"Know also that they recognise opposition between propositions, such as contradiction and contrariety. “Every animal is human” contradicts “Some animal is not human” and is contrary to “No animal is human,” even though propositions cannot be conceived as successively occupying a subject—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I135.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I136.lower.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I135.upper.01","volume":1,"page":135,"physical_pdf_page":148,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"المتقابلين قد يجتمعان في الوجود كبياض الرومي وسواد الحبشي وأما قيد وحدة الجهة فقد مر أنه احتراز عن خروج مثل الصغر والكبر وقد عرفت معنى امتناع اجتماعهما فخرج ما ليس كذلك (فإن كانا وجوديين فإن كان تعقل أحدهما بالقياس إلى الآخر فمتضايفان) كالأبوة والبنوة (وإلا) يكن تعقل","english":"—opposites can coexist in existence, as a Byzantine person’s whiteness and an Abyssinian person’s blackness do. The qualification “in one respect,” as already explained, prevents smallness and largeness from being excluded. You now know the meaning of their inability to coexist; what is not of this kind is excluded. “If both are positive, and conceiving one is relative to the other, they are correlatives,” such as fatherhood and sonship. “Otherwise,” if conceiving—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I135.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I134.upper.01","join_out":"I136.upper.01","editorial":"The ethnic examples reproduce the historical author’s example; they are not a claim about uniform characteristics of populations.","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I136.lower.01","volume":1,"page":136,"physical_pdf_page":149,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"فالجواب على ما ذكروه من وجهين أحدهما أن ذلك بحسب الاشتراك الاسمي كسائر النسب فإنها تكون في المفردات باعتبار الصدق أي الحمل على شيء وفي القضايا باعتبار الوجود أي تحققها في نفسها وثانيهما أن يجعل تقابل الإيجاب والسلب أعم مما في المفردات والقضايا ويعتبر موضوع القضية محلا لثبوت المحمول وعدم الثبوت على ما قيل من أن المتقابلين بالإيجاب والسلب إن لم يحتملا الصدق والكذب تقابلا بلا توسط كالفرسية واللافرسية وإلا فتركيب كقولنا زيد فرس وليس بفرس","english":"The answer, according to their account, takes two forms. First, the term is equivocal, as are the other relations: in simple expressions they concern applicability, meaning predication of something; in propositions they concern existence, meaning the propositions’ obtaining in themselves. Second, affirmative–negative opposition is made broad enough to include simple expressions and propositions, and the proposition’s subject is treated as the locus of the predicate’s obtaining and not obtaining. Thus it is said that, if the affirmative–negative opposites do not admit truth and falsity, their opposition is unmediated, as with equinity and nonequinity; otherwise it occurs through composition, as in “Zayd is a horse” and “Zayd is not a horse.”","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I136.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I135.lower.03","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I136.lower.02","volume":1,"page":136,"physical_pdf_page":149,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله وإن لم يكونا وجوديين الخ) عدم التعرض للعدميين مبني على ما ذهب إليه البعض من أنه","english":"On “If they are not both positive”: the absence of a discussion of two privatives rests on the view of some that—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I136.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I137.lower.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I136.upper.01","volume":1,"page":136,"physical_pdf_page":149,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"أحدهما بالقياس إلى الآخر (فمتضادان) كالسواد والبياض (وإن لم) يكونا وجوديين بل كان أحدهما وجوديا والآخر عدميا (فإن قيد) العدمي (بكون الموضوع مستعدا للوجودي) إما بحسب (شخصه) كعدم اللحية عن الأمرد (أو بحسب نوعه) كعدمها من المرأة (أو جنسه القريب) كالعمى للعقرب فإن البصر من شأن جنسه القريب أعني الحيوان (أو البعيد) كعدم الحركة الإرادية عن الجبل فإن جنسه البعيد أعني الجسم الذي فوق الجماد قابل للحركة الإرادية (فملكة وعدم وإلا) يقيد كذلك (فإيجاب وسلب) كالسواد واللاسواد هذا (وقد يشترط في التضاد غاية الخلاف) والبعد كالسواد والبياض لتباعدهما في الغاية دون الحمرة والصفرة إذ ليس بينهما ذلك الخلاف (ويخص) التضاد بهذا المعنى (باسم الحقيقي و) بالمعنى (الأول باسم المشهوري وقد يشترط في الملكة والعدم الاستعداد للوجودي في ذلك الوقت) كعدم اللحية عن الكوسج لا عن الأمرد الذي ليس من شأنه اللحية في ذلك الوقت (ويخص) هذا المعنى (باسم المشهوري والأول باسم الحقيقي ولا تقابل بالذات بين الوحدة والكثرة لتغاير موضوعهما) لأن موضوع الوحدة جزء موضوع الكثرة كما","english":"—one is not relative to the other, “they are contraries,” such as blackness and whiteness. “If they are not” both positive, but one is positive and the other privative, then, “if the privative is restricted to a subject receptive of the positive”—in respect of its “individual nature,” as absence of a beard in a beardless youth; “species,” as its absence in a woman; “proximate genus,” as blindness in a scorpion, since sight belongs to its proximate genus, animal; “or remote genus,” as absence of voluntary movement in a mountain, since its remote genus, body, above inanimate body, is receptive of voluntary movement—“they are possession and privation.” “Otherwise,” without that restriction, “they are affirmation and negation,” such as blackness and nonblackness. “Contrariety may require maximal difference” and distance, as with blackness and whiteness, which are maximally separated, unlike redness and yellowness. Contrariety in this sense “is called real,” and in the first sense “commonly recognised.” “Possession and privation may require receptivity to the positive at that time,” as absence of a beard in an adult unable to grow one, not in a youth who is not yet of an age to have one. This sense “is called commonly recognised, and the first real. Unity and multiplicity are not intrinsically opposed, because their subjects differ”: the subject of unity is a part of the subject of multiplicity, just as—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I136.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I135.upper.01","join_out":"I137.upper.01","editorial":"Examples of anatomy and animal capacities are the historical text’s own examples; no modern biological claim is made.","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I137.lower.01","volume":1,"page":137,"physical_pdf_page":150,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"لا تقابل بينها لأنهما إما أن يكون أحدهما مطلقا والآخر مضافا أو يكون كلاهما مضافين وفي الصورتين لا تقابل بينهما أما في الأولى فلضرورة صدق المطلق على المقيد وأما في الثانية فلأنهما يجتمعان في غير ما وقع الإضافة إليه كما في اللاسواد واللابياض فإنهما يصدقان في الحمرة مثلا فيصدق على الأحمر أنه لا أسود ولا أبيض قال في شرح المقاصد ما حاصله أن فيه نظرا لأن ما ذكر من اجتماع العدمين إنما يكون إذا لم يعتبر إضافة أحدهما إلى الآخر كما سبق وأما إذا اعتبر فيهما ذلك كالعمى واللاعمى والامتناع فيمتنع اجتماعهما ضرورة فتدبر","english":"—there is no opposition between them. Either one is unrestricted and the other relative, or both are relative; in neither case are they opposed. In the first, the unrestricted necessarily applies to the restricted. In the second, the two coexist in something other than that to which they are referred: nonblackness and nonwhiteness, for example, both apply to redness, so a red thing is neither black nor white. In Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid he objects, in effect, that the coexistence of two absences occurs only when neither is considered relative to the other, as already explained. When that relation is considered—as with blindness and nonblindness, and impossibility—their coexistence is necessarily impossible. Reflect.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I137.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I136.lower.final","readings":[{"locus":"والامتناع","status":"The isolated final example is printed in this form; the intended pairing requires checking rather than an invented additional term."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I137.lower.02","volume":1,"page":137,"physical_pdf_page":150,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله ويشترط في التقابل وحدة الموضوع) اعترض عليه بأنه إن أريد الوحدة بالشخص فممنوع لما نقل عن ابن سينا من أنه صرح بأن موضوع المتقابلين قد يكون واحدا بالشخص كالعدل والجور لزيد أو بالنوع كالرجلة والمرئية للإنسان أو بالجنس كالزوجية والفردية للعدد أو بأمر عارض كالخير والشر لشيء وإن أريد أعم من الوحدة بالشخص فمسلم ولا ينافي ذلك التغاير الكائن بين موضوع الوحدة والكثرة","english":"On “Opposition requires unity of subject”: it is objected that, if numerical unity is meant, this is denied. Avicenna is reported to have expressly stated that the subject of opposites may be numerically one, as with justice and injustice in Zayd; one in species, as with maleness and femaleness in the human being; one in genus, as with evenness and oddness in number; or one through something accidental, as with good and evil in a thing. If a unity broader than numerical unity is meant, it is conceded, but does not conflict with the difference between the subjects of unity and multiplicity.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I137.jpg","stage":69,"readings":[{"locus":"الرجلة والمرئية","status":"The two gender-related technical nouns are difficult in the scan; the translation gives their apparent intended sense, not a securely established spelling."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I137.lower.03","volume":1,"page":137,"physical_pdf_page":150,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله فإن الوحدة مقومة للكثرة) اعترض على هذا أيضا بأنه إن أريد أن ذات الكثرة مقومة بذات الوحدة فممنوع أما بحسب الخارج فلأنهما اعتباران عقليان وأما بحسب الذهن فلأنا نعقل الكثرة أي كون الشيء بحيث ينقسم بدون تعقل الوحدة أي كونه بحيث لا ينقسم وإن أريد أن معروض","english":"On “Unity constitutes multiplicity”: this too is challenged. If multiplicity itself is meant to be constituted by unity itself, that is denied. Externally, they are two intellectual considerations. Mentally, we conceive multiplicity—that a thing admits division—without conceiving unity—that it does not admit division. If instead the intended meaning is that the subject—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I137.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I138.lower.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I137.upper.01","volume":1,"page":137,"physical_pdf_page":150,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"أن الوحدة جزء الكثرة ويشترط في التقابل وحدة الموضوع (ولتقوم أحدهما بالآخر) فإن الوحدة علة مقومة للكثرة لكون الكثرة مجتمعة من الوحدات ولا شيء من المتقابلين كذلك أما فيما يكون أحدهما عدم الآخر فظاهر لأن العدم لا يكون مقوما وأما في التضايف فلأن المقوم للشيء متقدم عليه وجودا أو تعقلا والمتضايفان يكونان معا في التعقل والوجود وأما في التضاد","english":"—that unity is a part of multiplicity, whereas opposition requires unity of subject; and “because one is constituted through the other.” Unity is a constitutive cause of multiplicity, since multiplicity is an aggregate of units. Neither member of an opposed pair is related to the other in that way. This is evident where one is the absence of the other, since absence cannot constitute anything. In the case of correlatives, what constitutes a thing precedes it in existence or conception, whereas correlatives are simultaneous in conception and existence. As for contrariety—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I137.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I136.upper.final","join_out":"I138.upper.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I138.lower.01","volume":1,"page":138,"physical_pdf_page":151,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"الكثرة متقوم بمعروض الوحدة بمعنى أن الكثير يصدق على كل جزء منه أنه واحد كما هو ظاهر معنى اجتماع الكثرة من الوحدات فمسلم لكنه لا ينافي التقابل الذاتي بين الوحدة والكثرة العارضتين بل إنما ينافيه بين معروضيهما ولا نزاع فيه ألا ترى أنهم اتفقوا على أن المتقابلين بالذات إذا أخذا مع الموضوع كالبصير والأعمى لم يكن تقابلهما بالذات فكيف إذا أخذ نفس الموضوعين فإن قيل المراد هو الثاني وهو ينافي التقابل لأن كون أحد المعروضين متقوما بالآخر يستلزم اجتماعهما وهو يستلزم اجتماع الوصفين أجيب بأن اللازم هو اجتماعهما في الوجود لا في محل واحد والحاصل لا دليل على نفي تقابل الإيجاب والسلب بين الوحدة والكثرة بل تفسيرهم الكثرة بالانقسام والوحدة بسلبه ظاهر في ثبوت ذلك وأما اتفاقهم على نفي التقابل بينهما فمعناه أن الكثرة أي العدد لما كانت متقومة بالآحاد لم يكن بين العدد والواحد تقابل وهو ظاهر فيما هو جزء الكثرة وأما الوحدة التي ترد على الكثرة وتبطلها كما إذا جعلت مياه الكيزان في كوز واحد فقد يظهر تضادهما بناء على تواردهما على موضوع واحد هو ذلك الماء مع بطلان أحدهما بالآخر كذا ذكره بعض المحققين","english":"—of multiplicity is constituted by the subject of unity, in the sense that each part of what is many is called one, as is apparently meant by multiplicity’s being composed of units, this is conceded. But it does not exclude intrinsic opposition between unity and multiplicity as supervening attributes; it excludes it only between their subjects, which is undisputed. They agree that intrinsically opposed things, when taken together with their subjects—as “sighted” and “blind”—are not intrinsically opposed. How much more is this true when the subjects alone are considered! If it is said that the latter meaning is intended and does exclude opposition, because one subject’s being constituted by the other entails their coexistence, and therefore their attributes’ coexistence, the answer is that coexistence in existence follows, not coexistence in one subject. In sum, there is no proof excluding affirmative–negative opposition between unity and multiplicity; indeed, defining multiplicity as divisibility and unity as its negation apparently establishes it. Their agreement in denying opposition means that, since multiplicity—number—is constituted by units, number and a unit are not opposed. This is evident of the unit that forms part of multiplicity. As for a unity that comes upon multiplicity and abolishes it—for example, pouring the water of several jars into one jar—their contrariety may become apparent, since they successively occupy one subject, that water, and one abolishes the other. So states one of the investigators.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I138.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I137.lower.03","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I138.lower.02","volume":1,"page":138,"physical_pdf_page":151,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله وهما من لواحق الوجود) أما الأول فلضرورة امتناع تأثير المعدوم في الموجود وأما الثاني فبني على القول بامتناع كون الماهية مجعولة أي وبأن مجعوليتها جعلها موجودة على ما سبق تفصيله","english":"On “They are concomitants of existence”: the first follows from the evident impossibility of a nonexistent thing producing an existent. The second rests on denying that quiddity is made—that is, on holding that its being made means its being made existent, as previously explained in detail.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I138.jpg","stage":69,"editorial":"The gloss comments on the wording of the principal passage; its reference to “first” and “second” is preserved rather than silently harmonised.","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I138.lower.03","volume":1,"page":138,"physical_pdf_page":151,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قال العلة هي ما يحتاج إليه الخ) هذا هي","english":"On the author’s “A cause is what a thing needs”: this is—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I138.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I139.lower.01","readings":[{"locus":"هذا هي","status":"Apparent printed grammatical mismatch retained; the substantive sentence continues on the next page."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I138.upper.01","volume":1,"page":138,"physical_pdf_page":151,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"فلأن المقوم للشيء يجامعه والضد لا يجامع الضد وإنما قلنا بالذات لأن بينهما تقابل التضايف بواسطة ما عرض لهما من العلية والمعلولية لأن الوحدة علة مقومة للكثرة (فصل في العلة والمعلول) ولما كان وصف العلية والمعلولية من الأمور العامة أورده المصنف فيها وهما من لواحق الوجود والماهية وبينهما تقابل التضايف فلا يجتمعان في شيء واحد إلا باعتبارين (العلة هي ما يحتاج إليه الشيء)","english":"—what constitutes a thing coexists with it, whereas one contrary does not coexist with the other. We said “in themselves” because unity and multiplicity do have correlative opposition through the causality and being-caused that supervene upon them: unity is a constitutive cause of multiplicity. “Chapter on cause and effect.” Since causality and being-caused belong to the general attributes, the author discusses them here. They are concomitants of existence and quiddity. Their opposition is correlative, so they do not coincide in a single thing except under two considerations. “A cause is what a thing needs”—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I138.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I137.upper.01","join_out":"I139.upper.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I139.lower.01","volume":1,"page":139,"physical_pdf_page":152,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"العلة بالمعنى الأعم الذي ينقسم إلى العلل الأربعة وأما بالمعنى الأخص فهي تنصرف عند الإطلاق إلى الفاعل وهو ما يصدر عنه الشيء بالاستقلال أو بانضمام الغير إليه","english":"—cause in the broader sense, which divides into the four causes. In the narrower sense, an unqualified use refers to the agent: that from which a thing proceeds independently or with something else.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I139.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I138.lower.03","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I139.lower.02","volume":1,"page":139,"physical_pdf_page":152,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله وهي إما تامة أو ناقصة الخ) فيه بحث فإن العلة المعرفة هنا ليست مطلق العلة الشامل للتامة والناقصة كما سيصرح به «مد ظله» فالصواب ترك هذا التقسيم إلا أن يرتكب في الضمير الاستخدام فافهم","english":"On “It is either complete or incomplete”: this is questionable. The cause defined here is not cause without qualification encompassing complete and incomplete causes, as the commentator himself will expressly state. It would therefore be correct to omit this division, unless the pronoun is allowed to refer to the word in a different sense. Understand this.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I139.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I139.lower.03","volume":1,"page":139,"physical_pdf_page":152,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله بخلاف الوجوب الخ) أقول كما يصدق أنه يوجد الشيء مع المادة المصاحبة للصورة بالفعل يصدق أنه يجب معها كذلك إذ وجوب الشيء مع الشيء المصاحب لآخر أعم من أن يكون ناشئا منه أو من مصاحبه كما هو ظاهر فلا فرق وإن ادعى أن المتبادر من معية الوجوب للشيء ترتبه منه ففي تقييده بالقوة والفعل نظر فتدبر جدا","english":"On “Necessity is different”: I say that, just as the thing can be said to exist actually with matter accompanied by form, it can also be said to be necessary with it in that condition. A thing’s necessity together with something accompanied by another is broader than necessity arising from the former itself or from its companion. There is therefore no difference. If it is claimed that necessity’s accompanying a thing naturally suggests its resulting from that thing, then qualifying it by potentiality and actuality is questionable. Consider this very carefully.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I139.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I139.lower.04","volume":1,"page":139,"physical_pdf_page":152,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله البتة الخ) الحاصل أن الصورة يصدق فيها أنها كلما وجدت وجد الشيء معها بالفعل بخلاف المادة فإنه لا يصدق فيها ذلك بل الصادق هو أنها قد يكون إذا وجدت وجد الشيء معها بالقوة وذلك عند عدم مقارنتها للصورة وقد يكون إذا وجدت وجد معها بالفعل وذلك عند مقارنتها لها ولكن لا يخفى أن هذا إنما يصح لو كان المراد من المادة أعم من الهيولى ومثل الهيئة ومن الصورة أعم من الصورة والجوهرية ومثل الهيئة كما يدل عليه تمثيله «مد ظله» لهما بخشب السرير وهيئته","english":"On “Invariably”: the point is that, whenever form exists, the thing actually exists with it. The same is not true of matter. Sometimes, when matter exists, the thing exists with it potentially, namely when matter is not accompanied by form; and sometimes actually, namely when it is accompanied by form. But this works only if “matter” is understood broadly enough to include prime matter and such things [as the wood], and “form” broadly enough to include substantial form and such things as a configuration, as the commentator’s examples of a bed’s wood and configuration indicate—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I139.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I140.lower.01","readings":[{"locus":"أعم من الهيولى ومثل الهيئة ... ومن الصورة","status":"The printed coordination is compressed and possibly defective. The English marks the explanatory wood supply rather than claiming it is present in that clause."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I139.upper.01","volume":1,"page":139,"physical_pdf_page":152,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"وهي إما تامة أو ناقصة والناقصة إما جزء الشيء أي داخلة فيه أو أمر خارج عنه (فإن كانت داخلة ووجود الشيء معها إما بالفعل) كالهيئة للسرير (فهي) علة (صورية وإما بالقوة) كالخشب للسرير (فهي) علة (مادية) إذا لحقها الصورة يكون وجود المعلول معها بالفعل لا بالقوة فتدخل في تعريف الصورة فلا يكون مانعا وتخرج عن تعريف المادة فلا يكون جامعا بخلاف الوجوب فإنه بالنظر إلى المادة لا يكون بالقوة الآن يقال مرادهم أن الصورة ما يكون وجود الشيء معه بالفعل البتة والمادة ما يكون وجود الشيء معه بالقوة في الجملة (وإن كانت خارجة فالشيء إما بها فهي) علة (فاعلية) كالنجار للسرير (أو لها)","english":"—and it is either complete or incomplete. An incomplete cause is either a part of the thing, internal to it, or something external. “If it is internal and the thing exists with it actually,” as the configuration does in relation to a bed, “it is a formal” cause; “if potentially,” as wood does in relation to a bed, “it is a material” cause. Once form joins matter, the effect exists with matter actually rather than potentially; matter would therefore fall under the definition of form, making it insufficiently exclusive, and fall outside the definition of matter, making it insufficiently inclusive. Necessity is different: in relation to matter it is not potential. Unless it be said that they mean: form is that with which a thing invariably exists actually, whereas matter is that with which it exists potentially in some respect. “If the cause is external, then, if the thing exists through it, it is an efficient” cause, as the carpenter is for the bed; “or [the thing exists] for it”—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I139.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I138.upper.01","join_out":"I140.upper.01","readings":[{"locus":"بخلاف الوجوب ... الآن يقال","status":"The comparison involving necessity and the transition apparently إلا أن يقال require closer proof. The objection and proposed restrictive interpretation are translated without suppressing the textual difficulty."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I140.lower.01","volume":1,"page":140,"physical_pdf_page":153,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"وأما إذا كان المراد منهما الهيولى والصورة الجوهريتين فلا لامتناع انفكاكهما لما قالوا بالتلازم بينهما كما هو مقرر في محله فتدبر ثم قال في شرح المقاصد ما حاصله أن الجزء الأخير من الصورة المركبة يكون وجوب المعلول معه بالقوة لا بالفعل فيدخل في تعريف العلة المادية ويخرج من تعريف الصورية فينتقض التعريفان ولا يجوز أن يراد بالقوة الإمكان بحيث لا ينافي الفعل لأن الفساد حينئذ أظهر اه أقول فيه نظر أما أولا فلأن خروج جزء الشيء من تعريفه لازم فضلا من كونه قادحا فإما فخروج جزء الصورة عن تعريفها غير قادح بل القادح هو دخوله فيه وأما ثانيا فلأنه لا محذور في دخول جزء شيء في تعريف شيء آخر لجواز كون أحد الشيئين داخلا في الآخر إلا أن يقال باستلزامه هذا ضرورة أن مادة الشيء مثلا ليست جزءا من صورته بل كلاهما جزءان مستقلان للشيء فحينئذ ليس انتقاض تعريف المادة بذلك الجزء بل ينتقض حصر العلة الداخلة في المادية والصورية به فليتأمل وأما ثالثا فلأنه لم لا يجوز أن يكون التعريف للمادة والصورة الجوهريتين البسيطتين حتى لا يكون لها جزء فينتقض التعريف به إلا أن يقال فحينئذ تختص العلل الأربعة بالأجسام فلا يحصل مع أن المقصود بيانها مطلقا","english":"—whereas, if substantial prime matter and form are intended, it does not work: they cannot be separated, since their mutual entailment is established in its proper place. Reflect. He then says in Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid, in effect, that with the final part of a composite form the effect’s necessity is potential, not actual. That part would fall under the definition of material cause and outside that of formal cause, invalidating both definitions. “Potentiality” cannot mean possibility compatible with actuality, since the defect would then be more evident. End of quotation. I say: this is questionable. First, a thing’s part must fall outside its definition; far from being a defect, this is required. A part of form falling outside form’s definition is therefore unobjectionable; its inclusion would be the defect. Second, there is no difficulty in a part of one thing falling under the definition of another, since one thing may be internal to another—unless this is said to entail the difficulty here, since a thing’s matter is not a part of its form: the two are distinct parts of the thing. In that case, the part does not invalidate the definition of matter; it invalidates the restriction of internal causes to material and formal causes. Consider this. Third, why could the definitions not concern simple substantial matter and form, which have no part to invalidate the definitions? Unless it be replied that the four causes would then be restricted to bodies, whereas the intention is to explain them without restriction.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I140.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I139.lower.04","readings":[{"locus":"فإما فخروج / إلا أن يقال باستلزامه هذا / فلا يحصل","status":"Several short printed connectors are insecure; the three explicitly numbered objections are preserved without supplying a hidden argument."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I140.lower.02","volume":1,"page":140,"physical_pdf_page":153,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله كالجلوس عليه الخ) قيل لا دليل على انحصار الخارج فيما به الشيء وما لأجله سوى الاستقراء","english":"On “As sitting upon it”: it is said that there is no proof, apart from induction, restricting external causes to that through which a thing exists and that for whose sake it exists.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I140.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I140.lower.03","volume":1,"page":140,"physical_pdf_page":153,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله يفتقر إليهما في ماهيته الخ) وذلك لضرورة افتقار الكل إلى الجزء (قوله إذ لا يعقل إلا بهما الخ) أي إن كان تعقلا بالكنه لكن الأولى أن يقول كما في شرح المقاصد بهما أو بما لا ينتزع إلا منهما كالجنس والفصل انتهى لا يقال إذا كان الجنس غير المادة والفصل غير الصورة انتقض حصر جزء الشيء في المادة والصورة بهما لأنا نقول الجنس والفصل ليسا جزأين من النوع بل من حده كما سبق في بحث الماهية وقال في شرح المقاصد","english":"On “It needs them for its quiddity”: because a whole necessarily needs its part. On “It cannot be conceived without them”: that is, when conception grasps its full nature. It would be better to say, as in Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid, “through them, or through what can be abstracted only from them, such as genus and differentia.” End of quotation. Do not object that, if genus differs from matter and differentia from form, the restriction of a thing’s parts to matter and form is invalidated by them. We reply that genus and differentia are not parts of the species, but of its definition, as explained in the discussion of quiddity. In Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid he says—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I140.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I141.lower.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I140.upper.01","volume":1,"page":140,"physical_pdf_page":153,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"أي لأجلها (فهي) علة (غائية) كالجلوس عليه ويخص الأوليان أعني الصورية والمادية باسم علل الماهية لأن الشيء يفتقر إليهما في ماهيته لا في وجوده فقط إذ لا يعقل إلا بهما والأخريان أعني الفاعلية والغائية باسم علل الوجود لأن الشيء يفتقر إليهما في الوجود فقط ويعقل بدونهما (ومرجع الشروط","english":"—that is, for its sake: “it is a final” cause, as sitting upon the bed is. The first two, formal and material, are specially called causes of quiddity, because the thing needs them for its quiddity, not merely its existence; it cannot be conceived without them. The latter two, efficient and final, are called causes of existence, because the thing needs them only for existence and can be conceived without them. “Conditions are referred—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I140.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I139.upper.01","join_out":"I141.upper.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I141.lower.01","volume":1,"page":141,"physical_pdf_page":154,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"جعل الإمام عدم الانتقاض مبنيا على أنه لا تغاير بين الجنس والمادة وبين الفصل والصورة إلا بمجرد الاعتبار وهو إنما يتم لو كان الجنس مأخوذا من المادة والفصل من الصورة البتة حتى لا يكون للبسائط الخارجية كالمجردات أجناس وفصول وقد صرح المحققون بخلافه انتهى (أقول) هذا ينافي ما سبق منه آنفا من أنهما لا يفترقان إلا من المادة والصورة فافهم","english":"The Imām based the absence of a counterexample on there being no difference between genus and matter, or between differentia and form, except one of consideration. That would hold only if genus were invariably derived from matter and differentia from form, so that externally simple things, such as immaterial beings, had no genera or differentiae. The investigators have expressly stated otherwise. End of quotation. I say: this conflicts with his immediately preceding statement concerning their separation from matter and form. Understand this.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I141.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I140.lower.final","readings":[{"locus":"لا يفترقان إلا من المادة والصورة","status":"The final expression is provisionally transcribed; its precise verb and relation to the preceding quotation require enlargement and the preceding leaf."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I141.lower.02","volume":1,"page":141,"physical_pdf_page":154,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله لكونها داخلة فيما به الشيء الخ) لما سبق من أن الفاعل ما يصدر منه الشيء بالاستقلال أو بانضمام الغير قيل إن من الشروط ما هو عدمي كعدم المانع فيلزم استناد وجود المعلول إلى العلة المعدومة ضرورة انعدام الكل بانعدام الجزء وذلك باطل ضرورة لاستلزامه انسداد باب إثبات الصانع (أجيب) بأن المؤثر في وجود المعلول ليس هو العلة الفاعلية بجملتها بل ذات الفاعل وسائر ما يرجع إليها إنما هي شرائط التأثير لا أنها مؤثرة وقد يقال الشرط في الحقيقة أمر وجودي لكنه قد يختفي بحيث يكون ذلك الأمر العدمي كاشفا عنه مثلا شرط احتراق الخشبة ليس زوال الرطوبة بل اليبوسة التي ينبئ عنها زوال الرطوبة وهكذا","english":"On “Since they are included in that through which a thing exists”: as stated above, an agent is that from which a thing proceeds, either independently or with something else. It is objected that some conditions are privative, such as the absence of an impediment. The existence of the effect would consequently depend on a nonexistent cause, since a whole necessarily fails to exist when one of its parts fails to exist. That is plainly false, because it would close the route to establishing the Maker. The answer is that what produces the effect’s existence is not the efficient cause taken as a totality, but the agent’s own essence. The other things referred to it are conditions of its efficacy, not themselves producers. Alternatively, the condition is in reality something positive, but may be hidden, with a privative circumstance serving to disclose it. For example, the condition for wood’s burning is not the cessation of moisture, but the dryness indicated by that cessation; and similarly elsewhere.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I141.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I141.lower.03","volume":1,"page":141,"physical_pdf_page":154,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله والناقصة هي بعض ما يتوقف عليه الخ) فيه أن الأولى أن تقول بعض جميع ما يتوقف عليه الشيء لأن الناقصة بعض التامة والتامة ليست هي ما يتوقف عليه الشيء مطلقا بل هي جميع ما يتوقف عليه كما سبق ولعل الباعث له «مد ظله» على ذلك هو أن المصنف قال في شرح المقاصد إن التامة جميع ما يحتاج إليه الشيء والناقصة بعض ذلك انتهى","english":"On “An incomplete cause is some of what the thing depends on”: it would be better to say “some of the totality of what the thing depends on.” The incomplete cause is part of the complete cause; and the complete cause is not simply what a thing depends on without qualification, but everything on which it depends, as already stated. Perhaps what prompted the commentator—may his protection endure—to use this wording was the author’s statement in Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid: “The complete cause is everything the thing needs; the incomplete cause is some of that.” End of quotation—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I141.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I142.lower.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I141.upper.01","volume":1,"page":141,"physical_pdf_page":154,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"والآلات إلى الفاعل) فتندرج في الأقسام لكونها داخلة فيما به الشيء (وجميع ما يتوقف عليه الشيء) بمعنى ما ليس وراءه أمر آخر يحتاج إليه سواء كان ذلك الشيء مركبا أو بسيطا لا بمعنى أن يكون مركبا من عدة أمور البتة كما يشعر به عنوان الجمع (يسمى علة تامة) والناقصة هي بعض ما يتوقف عليه الشيء والتامة قد تكون هي الفاعل وحده كالبسيط الموجد للبسيط إيجابا وقد تكون هو مع الغاية كالبسيط الموجد للبسيط اختيارا فإن فعل المختار قد يكون لغرض يدعوه إليه وقد تكون هو مع المادة والصورة أيضا كالموجد للمركب","english":"—“and instruments to the agent.” They therefore fall within the divisions, since they are included in that through which a thing exists. “Everything on which a thing depends” means that beyond which there is no further thing it needs, whether the thing concerned is composite or simple. It does not mean that the cause must be composed of several things, as the collective expression might suggest. This “is called a complete cause”; an incomplete cause is some of what the thing depends on. The complete cause may be the agent alone, as when a simple thing necessarily produces a simple thing. It may be the agent together with an end, as when a simple thing voluntarily produces a simple thing: a voluntary agent’s act may be for a purpose that prompts it. It may also include matter and form, as in what produces a composite—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I141.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I140.upper.final","join_out":"I142.upper.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I142.lower.01","volume":1,"page":142,"physical_pdf_page":155,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"فظن الشارح «مد ظله» أن اسم الإشارة للمحتاج إليه الخ وليس كذلك بل هو إشارة إلى جميع ما يحتاج الخ كما ظهر ويمكن أن يجاب بأن مراده هو أن الناقصة بعض ما يحتاج إلى أجزائه الشيء فافهم","english":"The commentator—may his protection endure—therefore supposed that the demonstrative referred to “what is needed,” whereas it actually refers to “everything that is needed,” as has become clear. It may, however, be answered that he means that an incomplete cause is part of that whose constituent parts the thing needs. Understand this.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I142.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I141.lower.03","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I142.lower.02","volume":1,"page":142,"physical_pdf_page":155,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله لو جاز عدمه الخ) تحرير هذا الكلام على وجه يندفع عنه شكوك القاصرين هو أن الممكن إذا تمت علته بجميع جهات تأثيرها لا يخلو إما أن يجب وجوده عند ذلك التمام أولا وعلى الثاني إما أن لا يجب وجوده مطلقا أو يجب بعده فالأول هو المطلوب والثاني باطل لأنه خلاف المفروض أعني حصولها بجميع جهات التأثير وإليه الإشارة بقوله لأن المفروض الخ والثالث أيضا كذلك لاستلزامه الترجيح بلا مرجح وإليه الإشارة بقوله من غير أن يبقى شيء فلا يرد أن جواز عدمه عند التمام لا يستلزم وجوده بعده حتى يلزم الترجيح بلا مرجح وأقول الأظهر في الاستدلال أن يقال إذا تمت العلة بجميع جهات التأثير مع الترجيح لأحد","english":"On “If its nonexistence were possible”: the argument can be formulated so as to dispel the doubts of those whose understanding falls short. Once the possible thing’s cause is complete in every respect of efficacy, either its existence is necessary at that completion or it is not. In the latter case, either its existence never becomes necessary or becomes necessary later. The first alternative is the conclusion sought. The second is false because it contradicts the hypothesis that the cause obtains with every factor of efficacy; this is indicated by “since all the factors are assumed to obtain.” The third is also false, since it entails preference without a preferrer; this is indicated by “with nothing remaining.” Thus the objection does not arise that the possibility of its nonexistence at completion does not entail its later existence, and hence does not entail preference without a preferrer. I say: the clearer argument is to say that, when the cause is complete with all the factors of efficacy, together with preference for one—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I142.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I143.lower.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I142.ruled.01","volume":1,"page":142,"physical_pdf_page":155,"layer":"ruled","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله واحتياج الخ) لا يقال منشأ الاحتياج إلى كل واحدة هو عليتها له ومنشأ عدم الاحتياج إليهما علية الآخرين فلا استحالة في اجتماعهما لأنا نقول احتياج شيء إلى آخر في وجوده وعدم احتياجه إليه فيه متناقضان فلا يجتمعان مطلقا اه مواقف","english":"On “And dependence, etc.” Do not say: the ground of dependence on each one is its being a cause of the effect, while the ground of nondependence on the two is the causality of the others, so that their conjunction is not impossible. We reply that a thing’s dependence on another for its existence and its nondependence on that other for its existence contradict each other and cannot coexist under any circumstances. End. [Reference:] Mawāqif.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I142.jpg","stage":69,"editorial":"The printed pronouns are retained; this is a separately ruled quotation, not independently collated against al-Mawāqif.","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I142.upper.01","volume":1,"page":142,"physical_pdf_page":155,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"منه مع الغاية أو بدونها وإذا كانت العلة التامة مشتملة على المادة يمتنع تقدمها على المعلول واحتياج المعلول إليها ضرورة أن جميع أجزاء الشيء نفسه وإنما التقدم لكل جزء منها بمفرده وقال من أن العلة يجب تقدمها على المعلول ليس على إطلاقه بل العلة الناقصة أو التامة التي هي الفاعل وحده أو مع الغاية ومن هنا ظهر أن المعروف بما يحتاج إليه الشيء ليس مطلق العلة (وعند تمام الفاعل) بجميع جهات التأثير من الشرائط والآلات والقابل (يجب وجود المعلول) لأنه لو جاز عدمه لكان وجوده بعد ذلك ترجيحا بلا مرجح لأن المفروض حصول جميع جهات التأثير من غير أن يبقى شيء يوجب الترجيح (وامتناع الترجيح بلا مرجح)","english":"—from it, with or without an end. When a complete cause includes matter, it cannot precede the effect, nor can the effect depend on it, since all a thing’s parts together are the thing itself. Precedence belongs to each part separately. The statement that a cause must precede its effect is therefore not unrestricted: it concerns an incomplete cause, or a complete cause consisting of the agent alone or with an end. This also shows that what is defined as “what a thing needs” is not cause without qualification. “When the agent is complete” in every respect relevant to efficacy—conditions, instruments and a recipient—“the effect must exist.” If its nonexistence were possible, its subsequent existence would be a preference without a preferrer, since all the factors of efficacy are assumed to obtain, with nothing remaining to occasion the preference. “And the impossibility of preference without a preferrer”—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I142.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I141.upper.01","join_out":"I143.upper.01","readings":[{"locus":"وقال من أن العلة","status":"The connecting expression is difficult; the translation preserves the clearly stated qualification rather than asserting a secure diplomatic reading of those words."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I143.lower.01","volume":1,"page":143,"physical_pdf_page":156,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"الطرفين والوقت يجب وجود المعلول في ذلك الوقت إذ لو تخلف عنه لزم وجود المرجح بلا ترجيح والترجيح بلا مرجح فتأمل جدا","english":"—of the two alternatives and for the time, the effect must exist at that time. If it failed to occur, a preferrer would exist without preference, and preference without a preferrer. Consider this very carefully.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I143.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I142.lower.02","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I143.lower.02","volume":1,"page":143,"physical_pdf_page":156,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله يجب وجود الفاعل الخ) أي بجميع جهات التأثير (قوله فلو لم يجب وجود المؤثر الخ) قالوا إذا ثبت التلازم بين المؤثر التام ومعلوله ثبت أن لا تقدم له عليه بالزمان وإنما هو بالذات بمعنى الاحتياج إليه المصحح لأن يقال وجد المؤثر فوجد الأثر من غير عكس فإن قيل لو صح هذا لما جاز إسناد الحادث إلى القديم لتأخره عنه بالزمان قلنا من جملة جهات تأثيره في الحادث شرط حادث يقارنه كتعلق الإرادة بهذا وبعض الأوضاع عند الفلاسفة فالقديم مع جميع جهات تأثيره التي من جملتها ذلك الشرط إنما يتقدم الحادث بالذات لا بالزمان نعم يتقدم عليه ذات القديم بالزمان ولا نزاع فيه وما يقال من أن تأثير إيجاد العلة للمعلول عن وجودها ضروري وهو يستلزم تأخر وجود المعلول عنها فمردود بأنه إن أريد التأخر بالذات فغير المتنازع أو بالزمان فممنوع","english":"On “The agent must exist”: that is, with every factor of efficacy. On “Were the producer’s existence not necessary”: they say that, once mutual entailment between a complete producer and its effect is established, the producer has no temporal precedence over its effect. Its precedence is essential, meaning the effect’s need for it, which licenses “The producer existed, and therefore the effect existed,” but not the reverse. If it is objected that this would prevent a temporally originated thing from being ascribed to an eternal cause, since it comes after it in time, we reply that one of the factors of its efficacy in the originated thing is a concurrent originated condition—for example, the will’s attachment to this particular thing, or certain configurations according to the philosophers. The eternal cause together with every factor of efficacy, including that condition, precedes the originated thing essentially, not temporally. The eternal cause’s essence does, admittedly, precede it temporally; that is not disputed. The claim that a cause’s production of its effect must follow the cause’s existence, and that the effect’s existence must therefore follow it, is answered thus: essential posteriority is not disputed; temporal posteriority is denied.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I143.jpg","stage":69,"readings":[{"locus":"تأثير إيجاد العلة ... عن وجودها","status":"The phrase appears to express the posteriority of production to the cause’s existence; its first noun may be تأخر rather than تأثير. The interpretation is marked, not silently established as a variant."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I143.lower.03","volume":1,"page":143,"physical_pdf_page":156,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله بحركة يده مثلا الخ) وكذا الأب بحركاته وأفعاله يكون معدا للمادة لقبول الصورة وتنعدم تلك الأفعال لانعدام قصده ومشيئته فظهر أن كلا من الأب بالنسبة إلى الابن والبناء بالنسبة إلى البنية إنما يكون معدا مع","english":"On “By moving his hand, for example”: similarly, through his movements and acts the father prepares matter to receive a form. Those acts cease when his intention and volition cease. It is therefore clear that both the father in relation to the son and the builder in relation to the building are preparatory causes, together with—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I143.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I144.lower.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I143.upper.01","volume":1,"page":143,"physical_pdf_page":156,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"ضروري (وبالعكس) يعني عند وجود المعلول يجب تمام الفاعل (لأن الاحتياج) إلى المؤثر (من لوازم الإمكان) والإمكان من لوازم المعلول فلو لم يجب وجود المؤثر عند وجود المعلول لزم جواز وجود الملزوم بدون اللازم ولما كان في بعض الصور توهم وجود المعلول بدون العلة استدركه بقوله (ووجوده) أي المعلول (مع انعدامها) أي العلة (إنما يتصور في المعدات) لا في العلة المؤثرة الموجدة (كالابن بعد الأب والبناء بعد البناء) فإن البناء بحركة يده مثلا يحرك آلات من الخشبات واللبنات وتلك الحركات معدات لأوضاع مخصوصة بين تلك الآلات وتلك الأوضاع مستندة إلى علة فاعلية غير تلك الحركات لا يتصور وجودها بدونها وإنما تتصور بدون تلك الحركات (والمؤثر في الوجود) أي ما يفيد وجود الشيء قد يفيد بقاءه من غير افتقار إلى أمر آخر كالشمس تفيد","english":"—is self-evident. “And conversely”: when the effect exists, the agent must be complete, “because dependence” on a producer “is an inseparable concomitant of possibility,” and possibility is an inseparable concomitant of the effect. Were the producer’s existence not necessary when the effect exists, what entails a concomitant could exist without it. Since some examples seem to involve an effect existing without its cause, he qualifies this: “Its existence”—the effect’s—“despite the cause’s disappearance is conceivable only with preparatory causes,” not with the productive cause that brings it into existence. Examples are “a son after his father and a building after its builder.” By moving his hand, for instance, the builder moves implements consisting of timbers and bricks. Those movements prepare particular arrangements among the materials. The arrangements depend on an efficient cause other than those movements: they cannot exist without that cause, although they can exist without the movements. “What produces existence”—what confers the thing’s existence—may also confer its persistence without its needing anything further, as the sun confers—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I143.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I142.upper.01","join_out":"I144.upper.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I144.lower.01","volume":1,"page":144,"physical_pdf_page":157,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"الحركات والأفعال المخصوصة التي توجد قبل الابن والبناء وتنقضي عند وجودهما فلا يرد أنهما لو كانا معدين لزم انعدامهما عند معلوليهما كما هو شأن العلل المعدة فافهم ثم قال في شرح المقاصد هذا على رأي الفلاسفة وأما على رأي القائلين بإسناد الكل إلى الواجب تعالى بطريق الاختيار فالأمر بين","english":"—the particular movements and acts that occur before the son and building and cease when they exist. Thus it cannot be objected that, if the father and builder were preparatory causes, they would have to cease when their effects exist, as preparatory causes do. Understand this. He then says in Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid: this is according to the philosophers. According to those who ascribe everything to the Necessary Existent, exalted is He, through voluntary action, the matter is clear.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I144.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I143.lower.03","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I144.lower.02","volume":1,"page":144,"physical_pdf_page":157,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله وقد يفتقر البقاء الخ) أقول البقاء عندهم هو وجود الشيء بعينه في الزمان الثاني والثالث وهكذا فحينئذ إن أريد ببقاء الاشتعال في المثال الآتي هو أن المماسة الأولى أفادت وجود اشتعال ثم المماسة المتعاقبة بتعاقب الأسباب أفادت بقاء ذلك الاشتعال بعينه فغير مسلم لأن الظاهر هو أن كلا من المماسات مفيدة لاشتعال غير ما أفادته الأخرى فلا مفيد للبقاء بالمعنى المذكور وإن أريد بالبقاء الاستمرار بطريق تجدد الأمثال كما هو عند المتكلمين فلا نمنع أن المفيد لوجوده يغاير المفيد لبقائه بهذا المعنى وهو مجموع المماسات التي يفيد كل منها الوجود ابتداء فتفطنت حق التفطن","english":"On “Persistence may require [something further]”: I say that, for them, persistence is the existence of the very same thing at a second time, a third, and so on. If the coming example means that the first contact produces an ignition, and subsequent contacts arising through successive causes preserve that very ignition, this is not conceded. Apparently each contact produces an ignition other than that produced by the others; none therefore produces persistence in the stated sense. If persistence means continuity through the renewal of likenesses, as it does for the theologians, we do not deny that the producer of existence differs from the producer of persistence in that sense: the latter is the sum of the contacts, each of which initially produces existence. Attend to this with full discernment.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I144.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I144.lower.03","volume":1,"page":144,"physical_pdf_page":157,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله على معلول واحد بالشخص الخ) وهذا بخلاف الواحد بالنوع فإنه لا يمتنع اجتماع العلتين عليه بأن يقع بعض أفراده بعلة وبعض آخر بأخرى كالحرارة التي يقع بعضها بالنار وبعضها بالشمس فاحتياج الكل مغاير للمحتاج إلى الآخر فلا يلزم الاحتياج والاستغناء معا لا يقال المعلول النوعي إن احتاج لذاته إلى علة معينة امتنع استناده إلى غيرها أو لم يحتج كان غنيا عنها فلا يعرضه الاحتياج لأنا نقول عدم الاحتياج لذاته إلى معينة لا يستلزم استغناءه عن العلة مطلقا بل يجوز أن يحتاج لذاته إلى علة ما ولعارض إلى علة معينة فافهم","english":"On “A single numerically identical effect”: this differs from what is one in species. Two causes may act upon the latter, some of its individuals arising from one cause and others from another—for example, heat, some instances of which come from fire and others from the sun. What depends on one cause differs from what depends on the other; dependence and independence do not therefore coexist in one thing. Do not say: if the specific effect intrinsically needs a determinate cause, it cannot depend on another; and if it does not need that cause, it is independent of it and cannot acquire a need for it. We reply that not intrinsically needing a determinate cause does not entail absolute independence of a cause. It may intrinsically need some cause and, through an additional factor, need a determinate cause. Understand this.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I144.jpg","stage":69,"readings":[{"locus":"فاحتياج الكل مغاير للمحتاج إلى الآخر","status":"Compressed printed clause is uncertain; its sense distinguishes the individuals dependent on the respective causes."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I144.lower.04","volume":1,"page":144,"physical_pdf_page":157,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله أي على تقدير التوارد الخ) إشارة إلى رد من قال إنه كما يجوز احتياج الواحد بالنوع إلى علة لا بعينها جاز احتياج الواحد بالشخص إلى علة ما من العلتين من غير احتياج كل منهما حتى يلزم المحذور وحاصل الرد أن الكلام ليس في الاحتياج فإنه غير المتنازع بل في التوارد حيث يجوز في النوع","english":"On “That is, on the supposition of their convergence”: this answers the claim that, just as what is one in species may need an indeterminate cause, what is numerically one may need one or other of two causes without needing both, so that the alleged absurdity would not follow. The answer is that the discussion is not about dependence, which is undisputed, but about convergence: that is permissible in what is one in species—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I144.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I145.lower.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I144.upper.01","volume":1,"page":144,"physical_pdf_page":157,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"ضوء المقابل وبقاءه وقد يفتقر البقاء إلى أمر آخر فالمؤثر في الوجود (قد يغاير المؤثر في البقاء) كمماسة النار تفيد الاشتعال ثم يفتقر بقاء الاشتعال إلى استمرار المماسة بتعاقب الأسباب (ووحدة المعلول) بالشخص توجب وحدة الفاعل (لامتناع) توارد العلتين على معلول واحد بالشخص أي على تقدير التوارد","english":"—the light of what faces it and that light’s persistence. Persistence may instead require something further. Thus what produces existence “may differ from what produces persistence.” Contact with fire produces ignition; the ignition’s persistence then requires continued contact through successive causes. “The numerical unity of the effect” entails the unity of its agent, “because” two causes cannot converge upon a single numerically identical effect. That is, on the supposition of their convergence—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I144.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I143.upper.01","join_out":"I145.upper.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I145.lower.01","volume":1,"page":145,"physical_pdf_page":158,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"كما مر ويجتمع في الشخص وسيأتي في بحث أن وحدة الفاعل ليس لها مدخل في تشخص الحركة ما له تعلق بذلك إن شاء الله تعالى","english":"—as stated earlier, and it combines in the individual. The discussion showing that unity of agent plays no role in individuating motion will include something relevant to this, God willing.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I145.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I144.lower.final","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I145.lower.02","volume":1,"page":145,"physical_pdf_page":158,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله يلزم احتياجه الخ) وقد يستدل أيضا على امتناع التوارد المذكور بأن المعلول الواحد لو توقف على كل منهما لم يكن شيء منهما علة مستقلة بل جزء علة لأن معنى استقلال العلة هو أن لا تفتقر في التأثير إلى شيء آخر أو توقف على أحدهما فقط كانت هي العلة دون الأخرى أو لم يتوقف على شيء منهما لم يكن شيء منهما علة فتدبر","english":"On “It would have to need, etc.” Another argument against the concurrence of two causes is this: if the single effect depends upon each, neither is independently sufficient; each is only a part of a cause, since causal independence means needing nothing else to produce the effect. If it depends only upon one, that alone is the cause; if it depends upon neither, neither is a cause. Reflect.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I145.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I145.lower.03","volume":1,"page":145,"physical_pdf_page":158,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله كما زعم المخالف الخ) حيث ذهبت الفلاسفة إلى أن الواحد المحض بلا تعدد شروط وآلات واختلاف جهات واعتبارات لا يكون علة إلا لمعلول واحد (قوله لزم اتحاد السلسلة الخ) أي كون الموجودات بأسرها سلسلة واحدة يكون لها مبدأ واحد لا مبادئ متعددة وهو باطل فليتأمل","english":"On “As the opponent supposes, etc.” The philosophers hold that a sheer unity, without multiple conditions or instruments and without different aspects or considerations, causes only one effect. On “The series would be a single series, etc.”: all existents would form one series with one principle, not several principles. This is false. Let it be considered.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I145.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I145.lower.04","volume":1,"page":145,"physical_pdf_page":158,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله ولزم في كل موجودين الخ) هذا لازم من المقدم لا من التالي فإنه لازم أعم لجواز وحدة السلسلة بالمعنى السابق مع عدم ما فرض من صدور الواحد من الواحد وذلك بأن تكون جميعها معلولا لعلة واحدة إلا أن يقال وحدة السلسلة الملازمة لصدور الواحد من الواحد تستلزم العلية فيما بين كل اثنين فليتدبر","english":"On “Any two existents would therefore, etc.” This follows from the antecedent, not the consequent, for the consequent is broader: a single series in the stated sense is possible without only one proceeding from one, if all its members are effects of one cause. Unless it be said that the particular unity of series which follows from only one proceeding from one entails a causal relation between every two members. Reflect.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I145.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I145.lower.05","volume":1,"page":145,"physical_pdf_page":158,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله لو لم يكن في المعلول الأول الخ) وقد يقال أيضا ذلك إنما يلزم في ما يصدر","english":"On “Only if the first effect contained no, etc.” It may also be said that this consequence applies only to what proceeds—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I145.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I146.lower.01","readings":[{"locus":"ذلك إنما يلزم في ما يصدر","status":"The last small-font line is faint; the opening of the objection continues on I:146."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I145.paratext.01","volume":1,"page":145,"physical_pdf_page":158,"layer":"paratext","attribution":"Printed gathering signature","arabic":"(١٠ ـ تقريب أول)","english":"[Gathering signature:] 10 — Taqrīb, first part.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I145.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I145.upper.01","volume":1,"page":145,"physical_pdf_page":158,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"يلزم احتياجه إلى كل من العلتين لكونه علة واستغناؤه عن كل منهما لكون الأخرى مستقلة بالعلية و(الاحتياج والاستغناء معا ممتنع ضرورة) (ولا عكس) عندنا بمعنى وحدة الفاعل لا توجب وحدة المعلول (لاستناد الكل إلى الواجب تعالى ابتداء) من غير توسط شيء كما زعم المخالف (والاستدلال) على ما عندنا (بأنه لو لم يصدر من الواحد إلا الواحد) لما صدر عن المعلول الأول إلا الواحد وهو الثاني وعنه واحد هو الثالث وهلم فإذن (لزم اتحاد السلسلة) لزم في كل موجودين فرضا أن يكون أحدهما علة للآخر والآخر معلولا له بواسطة أو بدونها فيلزم ثبوت (العلية فيما بين كل شيئين) حتى بينك وبين هذا الحجر (ضعيف) لا يصلح للاستدلال لأن ذلك إنما يلزم لو لم يكن في المعلول الأول مع وحدة الذات","english":"—it would have to need each of the two causes because each is a cause, and to be independent of each because the other is independently sufficient. “Need and independence together are self-evidently impossible. The converse does not hold,” on our account: unity of agent does not entail unity of effect, “since everything depends directly upon the Necessary Existent,” without the intermediary posited by the opponent. “The argument” for our position that “if only one proceeded from one,” the first effect would produce only one—the second—and it only a third, and so on, “the series would have to be a single series,” and any two existents would therefore have to stand as cause and effect, directly or indirectly, entailing “causality between every two things,” even between you and this stone, “is weak” and unsuitable as a proof. That consequence follows only if the first effect, despite the unity of its essence, contains no—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I145.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I144.upper.final","join_out":"I146.upper.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I146.lower.01","volume":1,"page":146,"physical_pdf_page":159,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"عن الواحد مع المعلول أو بتوسط شيء آخر وهكذا لم لا يجوز أن يصدر عن المعلول الأول ثان وعن المبدأ الأول بتوسطه ثالث وبتوسط الأول والثاني رابع وهكذا عن كل بتوسط ما فوقه أو ما تحته وعن المبدأ بتوسط طائفة جملة وفرادى شيء فيكون هناك سلاسل غير محصورة","english":"—from the one together with its effect, or through another thing, and so on. Why could a second effect not proceed from the first, a third from the First Principle through the first, and a fourth through the first and second? Something could similarly proceed from each through those above or below it, and from the Principle through groups, jointly or separately. There would then be indefinitely many series.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I146.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I145.lower.05","readings":[{"locus":"عن الواحد مع المعلول أو بتوسط شيء آخر","status":"The opening follows the faint final line on I:145; the exact introductory formulation remains provisional."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I146.lower.02","volume":1,"page":146,"physical_pdf_page":159,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله وإنما لم يجعل الواجب الخ) جواب عما اعترض به وهو أنه لو كفى مثل هذه الكثرة في جواز كون الواحد مصدرا للمعلولات الكثيرة لكفى أن يجعل الواجب مصدرا لها باعتبار ما له من كثرة السلوب والإضافات (قوله إنما تعقل بعد ثبوت الغير الخ) اعترض بأن تعقل السلوب إنما يتوقف على تعقل الغير لا على ثبوته والمتوقف عليهما ثبوت الغير لا تعقله فلا دور قال في شرح المقاصد والجواب أن المراد أنه لا يصح الحكم بالسلوب في نفس الأمر إلا بعد ثبوت الغير ضرورة اقتضاء السلب مسلوبا فلا يصح الحكم باستناد شيء إليها للزوم الدور انتهى","english":"On “The Necessary Existent is not similarly made, etc.” This answers the objection that, if such multiplicity suffices for one thing to produce many effects, the Necessary Existent’s multiple negations and relations should suffice for Him to produce them. On “Negations are conceived only after another is established, etc.” The objection is that conceiving a negation requires only conceiving the other, not its actual existence. What depends on the negative predicates is the other’s existence, not its conception, so there is no circle. Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid answers: the point is that the negations cannot truly be affirmed in reality before the other exists, since negation requires something negated. Something cannot therefore be grounded in those negations without circularity. End.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I146.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I146.lower.03","volume":1,"page":146,"physical_pdf_page":159,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"أقول فيه بحث إذ يصح الحكم على زيد مثلا بأنه ليس بشريك للباري في نفس الأمر ولا يقتضي ذلك ثبوت المسلوب الذي هو شريك الباري ضرورة ولعل الأمر منه «مد ظله» بالتدبر إشارة إلى جميع ما ذكر ثم أقول الحق في الجواب عن أصل الاعتراض هو أن سلب النقص والاتصاف بالوجوب ونحو ذلك كلها من الاعتبارات التي لا تحقق لها ولا تمايز بينها في الأعيان ولو سلم فهي لا تلحق الواحد من حيث هو واحد بل تستدعي كثرة تلحقها فإن السلب يفتقر إلى مسلوب ومسلوب عنه يتقدمانه والاتصاف إلى موصوف","english":"I say: this is questionable. One may truly affirm that Zayd, for example, is not a partner of the Creator without this requiring an actual entity answering to “partner of the Creator.” Perhaps the commentator’s instruction to reflect refers to all these considerations. I further say: the proper answer to the original objection is that denying deficiency, qualifying something by necessity, and the like are all considerations with no distinct extramental actuality. Even if that were granted, they do not attach to the one precisely as one, but presuppose a multiplicity to which they attach. Negation requires something negated and something from which it is negated, both prior to it; qualification requires a subject—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I146.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I147.lower.01","readings":[{"locus":"سلب النقص والاتصاف بالوجوب","status":"The first short noun and the qualification following it are read provisionally from the scan."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I146.upper.01","volume":1,"page":146,"physical_pdf_page":159,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"كثرة بحسب الجهات والاعتبارات على ما ذكروا أنه إذا صدر عن المبدأ الأول الذي ليس فيه تكثر بجهات شيء كان ذلك الشيء واحدا بالذات لكن يعقل له بحسب الاعتبارات المختلفة أمور هي الوجود والهوية والإمكان والوجوب بالغير فيجوز أن يصدر عنه بحسب تلك الاعتبارات أمور متكثرة تصير ابتداء سلاسل وإنما لم يجعل الواجب مبدأ للممكنات باعتبار ما له من كثرة السلوب لأن السلوب إنما تعقل بعد ثبوت الغير فلو علل بها كان دورا فتدبر (وتمسك المخالف) يعني الفلاسفة (بأنه لو صدر عنه) أي عن الواحد الحقيقي (شيئان فمصدريته","english":"—multiplicity in aspects and considerations. They explain that when something proceeds from the First Principle, which has no multiplicity of aspects, the thing produced is one in essence but admits different considerations: existence, identity, possibility and necessity through another. Different things may proceed from it according to those considerations, becoming beginnings of different series. The Necessary Existent is not similarly made the principle of contingents through His multiple negative predicates, because negations are conceived only after another thing is established. Explaining that other through the negations would be circular. Reflect. “The opponent”—the philosophers—“argues that, if two things proceeded from a genuine unity, its being the source—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I146.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I145.upper.01","join_out":"I147.upper.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I147.lower.01","volume":1,"page":147,"physical_pdf_page":160,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"وصفة كذلك ولا يكفي المسلوب عنه فقط أو الموصوف فقط فهي لا تجعل الواجب الواحد متكثرا بحيث يكون مبدأ للمعلولات المتكثرة بخلاف المصدرية كما يأتي تحقيقها فافهم","english":"—and a qualification, likewise prior to it. The subject of negation or qualification alone is not enough. These considerations therefore do not make the one Necessary Existent multiple in a way that could ground many effects. Sourcehood differs, as will be explained. Understand this.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I147.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I146.lower.03","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I147.lower.02","volume":1,"page":147,"physical_pdf_page":160,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله فلا يكونان نفسه الخ) إن أراد أنه لا يكونان مع بقاء الاثنينية بينهما نفس الواحد لاستلزامه التركب المنافي لوحدته فمسلم لكن قوله وذلك ظاهر يفيد بظاهره أنه فساد غير ما يترتب من الشقوق الآتية وليس كذلك وإن أراد أنه ليس شيء منهما نفسه فيرد أن تغايرهما لا ينافي كون أحدهما نفسه والآخر غيره وإن أراد أنه لا يجوز أن يكون كل منهما نفسه لاستلزامه اتحاد الاثنين فمسلم أيضا لكن امتناع ذلك لا يوجب الانحصار في الشقوق الآتية لبقاء احتمال كون أحدهما نفسه والآخر خارجا فلا يتم قوله فإما أن يكون شيء منهما الخ وسيجيء منا تحرير هذا الاستدلال على وجه هو مقصود الفلاسفة","english":"On “They cannot both be the thing itself, etc.” If this means that, while remaining two, they cannot both be identical with the one—because that would involve composition incompatible with its unity—it is admitted. But “that is evident” appears to present a difficulty distinct from the consequences of the following alternatives, whereas it is not distinct. If he means that neither can be the thing itself, their difference does not exclude one being the thing itself and the other different. If he means that each cannot be identical with it, since that would identify the two, this too is admitted; but its impossibility does not exhaust the possibilities subsequently listed. One might still be the thing itself and the other external. Thus “either something of them, etc.” is not exhaustive. We will reformulate the argument in accordance with the philosophers’ intention.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I147.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I147.lower.03","volume":1,"page":147,"physical_pdf_page":160,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله فإما أن يكون شيء منهما الخ) أي سواء كان أحدهما أو كليهما كما يفسر الشارح «مد ظله» ما في المتن بذلك عن قريب (قوله تركب الواحد فقط) أي من غير لزوم التسلسل وذلك في صورة دخول كليهما فيه فيلزم تركبه منهما ومما زاد فيهما لم يفرض الدخول فظهر أن التركب اللازم من هذا أشد مما سبق فتدبر","english":"On “Either something of them, etc.”: whether one or both, as the commentator shortly explains. On “Composition alone”: without regress. This is the case where both enter its constitution, making it composed of them and of any further constituent not included in the supposition of entry. The composition entailed here is thus more pronounced than the preceding one. Reflect.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I147.jpg","stage":69,"readings":[{"locus":"ومما زاد فيهما لم يفرض الدخول","status":"The brief account of an additional constituent is insecure and requires a second visual reading."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I147.lower.04","volume":1,"page":147,"physical_pdf_page":160,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله أو تركب هو وتسلسلا الخ) وذلك في صورة دخول إحداهما وخروج الأخرى فيلزم التركب من الدخول والتسلسل من الخروج فظهر أن في الكلام لفا ونشرا من غير ترتيب ثم أقول يلزم من هذا ومما سبق فساد آخر لم يتعرضوا له وهو أن المصدرية لما كانت حاصلة عند الصدور لا قبله تكون لا محالة متأخرة من ذات المصدر فكيف يتصور كونها نفسه أو من ذاتياته وداخلة فيه وهل هذا إلا حكم بتقدم","english":"On “Or both composition and regress, etc.” This is where one enters and the other remains external: entry entails composition and externality entails regress. The sentence thus distributes its consequences without preserving their order. I further say: this and the foregoing entail another difficulty they do not mention. Since sourcehood obtains when production occurs, not beforehand, it must be posterior to the source’s essence. How can it be identical with that essence, or essential to it and included within it? Would that not make—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I147.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I148.lower.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I147.upper.01","volume":1,"page":147,"physical_pdf_page":160,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"لهذا غير مصدريته لذلك) لأن المفهوم من هذا غير المفهوم من ذلك فلا يكونان نفسه وذلك ظاهر فإما أن يكون شيء منهما داخلا فيه أولا بأن كان كلاهما خارجا عنه (فإن دخل شيء منهما) سواء كان أحدهما أو كلاهما (فيه) أي في الواحد الحقيقي (تركب) الواحد فقط أو تركب هو وتسلسل المصدريات أيضا","english":"—of this differs from its being the source of that.” The concept of the former differs from that of the latter; they cannot therefore both be the thing itself. That is evident. Either something of them enters the thing’s constitution or neither does, both being external. “If either”—one or both—“enters it,” the genuine unity “becomes composite”: either composition alone follows, or both composition and a regress of source-relations follow.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I147.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I146.upper.01","join_out":"I148.upper.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I148.lower.01","volume":1,"page":148,"physical_pdf_page":161,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"الشيء على نفسه وتأخره منه وهو ضروري البطلان فتدبر (قوله تسلسل المصدريات الخ) أي فقط فافهم","english":"—a thing prior and posterior to itself? This is self-evidently false. Reflect. On “A regress of source-relations”: that alone. Understand this.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I148.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I147.lower.04","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I148.lower.02","volume":1,"page":148,"physical_pdf_page":161,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله ولو سلم أنها أمر حقيقي الخ) وجه التسليم هو ما قرروه من أن المصدرية كما تطلق على الأمر الإضافي الذي يعرض للعلة والمعلول من حيث اعتبار العقل نسبة أحدهما إلى آخر وليس كلامنا فيه كذلك تطلق على معنى حقيقي هو كون العلة مشتملة على معنى يصح صدور المعلول منها وكلامنا فيه والمراد بهذا المعنى خصوصية لها بالقياس إلى الأثر بحسبها يجب الأثر وقالوا إن هذا المعنى وجودي ضرورة فإنا إذا سلمنا حركات متعددة فما لم تحصل لنا خصوصية بالقياس إلى كل حركة وأقلها إرادتها لم تصدر منا تلك الحركة وهكذا سائر العلل المؤثرة","english":"On “Even if it is granted to be real, etc.” The concession is explained by their distinction between two meanings of sourcehood. It can signify the relational consideration attaching to cause and effect when the intellect considers one relative to the other; that is not the present subject. It can also signify a real feature: the cause contains something in virtue of which its effect can proceed from it. This is the subject at issue—a specific determination of the cause relative to the effect, by virtue of which the effect necessarily follows. They hold this to be evidently positive: even when we are capable of several motions, a particular motion does not proceed from us without a particular determination relative to it, at least the volition to perform it. The same applies to other efficacious causes.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I148.jpg","stage":69,"readings":[{"locus":"إذا سلمنا حركات متعددة","status":"The verb may be علمنا rather than سلمنا. The English follows the example of several possible motions, without a settled transcription of that word."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I148.lower.03","volume":1,"page":148,"physical_pdf_page":161,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله مغايرة له ضرورة أنها نسبة الخ) أورد عليه أنه ليس كلامنا في تلك النسبة الإضافية بل في الأمر الحقيقي الذي مر بيانه آنفا وهو لا نسلم مغايرته له لجواز كونه نفسه وأيضا ينافي هذا التسليم السابق من الشارح من أنها أمر حقيقي فتدبر","english":"On “Distinct from it, since it is a relation, etc.” The objection is that the discussion concerns not that relational consideration but the real feature just explained. Its distinction from the cause is not conceded, for it may be identical with the cause itself. Moreover, this reply conflicts with the commentator’s preceding concession that sourcehood is real. Reflect.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I148.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I148.upper.01","volume":1,"page":148,"physical_pdf_page":161,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(وإلا) يدخل فيه شيء منهما فيكون له مصدر وعنه فننقل الكلام إلى مصدريته له فحينئذ (تسلسل) المصدريات فقط والكل باطل (ورد) هذا التمسك (بأنه إن أريد التغاير بحسب الخارج فممنوع لأنها) أي المصدرية (اعتبار عقلي) لا تحقق لها في الأعيان أو بحسب الذهن فلا محذور في كونهما نفسه بحسب الخارج على أنهما لما كانت من الاعتبارات التي لا وجود لها في الخارج كانت غير محتاجة إلى علة توجدها فلا يكون الذات مصدرا لها فلا يكون هناك مصدرية أخرى فتسلسل (ولو سلم أنها أمر حقيقي رد أيضا بأنه يرد على صدور الواحد) فإنها لو كانت متحققة في الخارج فالواحد الحقيقي إذا صدر عنه شيء فقد تحقق هناك مصدرية مغايرة له ضرورة أنها نسبة بينه وبين ذلك الشيء فلا","english":"“Otherwise,” if neither enters its constitution, each has a source from which it proceeds. The question transfers to that source’s being its source, and “a regress” of source-relations alone follows. All these consequences are false. “The reply” to the argument is that, “if extramental difference is intended, it is denied, since sourcehood is an intellectual consideration” with no actuality among external entities. If conceptual difference is intended, both may nevertheless be externally identical with the essence. Moreover, because source-relations are considerations without external existence, they need no cause to produce them. The essence is not their productive source, so no further source-relation and regress arise. “Even if sourcehood is granted to be real, the same argument applies to the production of one effect.” If sourcehood existed externally, then whenever one thing proceeded from a genuine unity there would be a source-relation distinct from that unity, since it is a relation between it and its effect. It cannot—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I148.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I147.upper.01","join_out":"I149.upper.01","readings":[{"locus":"فيكون له مصدر وعنه / فتسلسل","status":"The opening pronouns and a short final connective are compressed in the print; the alternative leading to a regress is retained."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I149.lower.01","volume":1,"page":149,"physical_pdf_page":162,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله فإن دخلت الخ) فيه أن الكلام لما كان في الأمر الحقيقي جاز أن يكون ذلك الأمر في صدور الواحد نفس ذلك المصدر الواحد فلا تركب ولا تسلسل بخلاف ما إذا صدر عنه شيئان فيكون هناك خصوصيتان وجوديتان متغايرتان فإما أن يكونا معا نفس المصدر فتركب أو يكون كل نفسه فيتحد الاثنان أو يكون أحدهما نفسه والأخرى غيره فيلزم التسلسل أو يكون إحداهما داخلة والأخرى خارجة فيلزم التركب والتسلسل أو يكونا معا داخلين فالتركب أو خارجين فالتسلسل هذا هو التحرير المعهود الموافق لمقصودهم فتأمله","english":"On “If those aspects enter, etc.” The point is that, since the discussion concerns something real, in the production of one effect that real feature may be the single source itself, with neither composition nor regress. But if two effects proceed from it, there are two distinct positive determinations. If both together are the source itself, composition follows; if each is the source itself, the two become identical. If one is the source itself and the other something different, regress follows. If one is internal and the other external, both composition and regress follow; if both are internal, composition; if both external, regress. This is the familiar formulation agreeing with their intention. Consider it.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I149.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I149.lower.02","volume":1,"page":149,"physical_pdf_page":162,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله ويبعد أن يجعل من معارك الخ) فإنه يفضي إلى أن يكون النزاع فيما بينهم لفظيا والنزاع اللفظي لا ينبغي أن يتمادى فيه هذا التمادي (قوله فإن كان البسيط قابلا الخ) أي قابلا لشيء وفاعلا لذلك الشيء فإنه الذي يمنعونه دون مطلق","english":"On “It scarcely deserves to be a major field of dispute, etc.” It would make the disagreement merely verbal, and a verbal dispute should not be pursued to such lengths. On “If a simple thing is receptive, etc.”: receptive of a thing and productive of that same thing, for that is what they disallow—not simply—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I149.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I150.lower.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I149.upper.01","volume":1,"page":149,"physical_pdf_page":162,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"فتكون نفسه فإن دخلت تركب وإلا تسلسل (وقولهم المراد) بأن الواحد لا يصدر عنه إلا الواحد (أنه كما تكثر المعلول تكثر الفاعل ولو بالحيثية ضرورة أن فاعليته لهذا) الشيء (غير فاعليته لذلك) الشيء وإذا كان تكثر المعلول مستلزما لتكثر الفاعل كان وحدة الفاعل مستلزمة لوحدة المعلول بحكم عكس النقيض (لا يفيد) هذا القول (شيئا) نافعا ويبعد عن أن يجعل من معارك الآراء (ولا يوافق أيضا ما بنوا) أي بنوه (عليه) أي على أن الواحد الحقيقي لا يصدر عنه إلا الواحد (من امتناع تعدد آثار البسيط) فإنه على هذا القول يجوز أن يصدر عن البسيط مهما نزل أشياء فيكون عليته لكل مفهوم اعتباريا مغايرا لعليته للآخر ولا يقدح ذلك في بساطته الحقيقية وإلا لما جاز أن يصدر عنه شيء أصلا لأن عليته لذلك الشيء أمر مغاير لذاته بالضرورة بحسب التعقل","english":"—so that it would be the thing itself. If those [aspects] enter its constitution, it is composite; otherwise a regress follows. “Their explanation that the intended meaning” of “only one proceeds from one” is that “as effects multiply, the agent multiplies, at least in respect, since its causing this thing differs from its causing that thing”—so that multiplicity of effects implies multiplicity in the agent, and by contraposition unity in the agent implies unity of effect—“contributes nothing” useful, and scarcely deserves to be a major field of dispute. “Nor does it agree with what they build upon the principle,” namely, “the impossibility of a simple thing’s having several effects.” On this interpretation, many things could proceed from a simple thing, however far the sequence extended. Its causing each would be a conceptual relation different from its causing the others, without impairing its real simplicity. Otherwise nothing could proceed from it at all, since its causing that thing is necessarily conceptually distinct from its essence.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I149.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I148.upper.final","readings":[{"locus":"مهما نزل","status":"The short phrase is retained literally; its intended qualification of the simple cause requires a second source reading."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I149.upper.02","volume":1,"page":149,"physical_pdf_page":162,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(ومن أن الفاعل البسيط) الحقيقي (لا يكون قابلا) لأن الفعل والقبول أثران فإن كان البسيط قابلا وفاعلا فقد صدر عنه أثران وهو باطل فإن الواحد لا يصدر عنه إلا الواحد وعلى هذا القول لا يمتنع من","english":"They also build upon it “that a genuinely simple agent cannot be receptive”: acting and receiving are two effects, so a simple thing which both acts and receives would have two effects, contrary to the principle that only one proceeds from one. On the proposed interpretation, however, there is no obstacle to—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I149.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I150.upper.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I150.lower.01","volume":1,"page":150,"physical_pdf_page":163,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"كون البسيط قابلا وفاعلا يدل عليه الاستدلال بتنافي الوجوب والإمكان فإنهما إنما يتنافيان بالنسبة لشيء واحد وكذا قولهم في الرد عليهم إنه لو صح ذلك لزم أن لا يكون الواحد قابلا لشيء وفاعلا لآخر","english":"—a simple thing’s being receptive and active in any respect. The appeal to the incompatibility of necessity and possibility shows this, since they conflict only in relation to the same thing. So does the objection to them that, if the argument were sound, one thing could not receive one thing and produce another.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I150.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I149.lower.02","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I150.lower.02","volume":1,"page":150,"physical_pdf_page":163,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله بأن الفاعل إذا أخذ وحده الخ) لا يخفى مخالفة هذا الترديد فإن الكلام كما صرح به إنما هو في الواحد البسيط الحقيقي ولا نسلم أن البسيط المذكور إذا فرض كونه فاعلا فلا يجب معه وجود المفعول إذ المفروض استقلاله في إيجاده وإلا لم يكن بسيطا حقيقيا بخلاف ما إذا فرض قابلا فإنه لا يجب معه المقبول ولو مع استقلاله في القابلية فتدبر جدا","english":"On “When the agent is taken alone, etc.” This division into alternatives plainly misses the case. As explicitly stated, the discussion concerns a genuinely simple unit. We do not concede that, when it is supposed to act, its effect need not exist with it: independent production is assumed, or else the cause would not be genuinely simple. But if it is supposed to receive, the received thing does not necessarily exist with it, even when its receptivity is independent. Reflect very carefully.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I150.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I150.lower.03","volume":1,"page":150,"physical_pdf_page":163,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله فلا فرق بينهما الخ) أقول قد ظهر الفرق آنفا وأيضا أخذ القابل مع جميع ما يتوقف عليه وجود المقبول إما بأن يكون وحده كافيا في وجوده فهو تمام العلة وليس قابلا وإما بأن يكون مع غيره كذلك فليس من المتنازع ويقرب مما ذكرنا ما يصرح به قريبا في معنى التسليم فتدبره","english":"On “There is no difference between them, etc.” I say: the difference has just become apparent. Furthermore, taking the recipient with everything on which the received thing depends either means that the recipient alone suffices—in which case it is the complete cause, not merely a recipient—or that it suffices together with something else, which is not the disputed case. His forthcoming explanation of the concession approaches what we have said. Reflect on it.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I150.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I150.lower.04","volume":1,"page":150,"physical_pdf_page":163,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله بالإمكان العام الخ) لأن معنى قابلية الشيء لشيء هو أنه لا يمتنع حصوله فيه ومنع بأن معناها هو أنه لا يمتنع حصوله ولا عدم حصوله فيه وهو معنى الإمكان الخاص","english":"On “Possibility in the general sense, etc.” For receptivity to something means that its occurrence in the recipient is not impossible. The objection is that receptivity means that neither its occurrence nor its nonoccurrence is impossible, which is possibility in the specific sense.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I150.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I150.upper.01","volume":1,"page":150,"physical_pdf_page":163,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"كون البسيط مما يصدر عنه شيئان كما مر آنفا (وقد يستدل) على أن الفاعل لا يكون قابلا (بأن نسبة الفاعل) إلى المفعول (بالوجوب) ونسبة (القابل) إلى المقبول (بالإمكان) فيكون قبول الشيء وفاعليته له متنافيين لتنافي لازميهما أعني الوجوب والإمكان لا يجتمعان (ورد) بأن الفاعل إذا أخذ وحده لم يجب معه وجود المفعول كان القابل وحده لا يجب معه وجود المقبول وإذا أخذا مع جميع ما يتوقف عليه وجود المفعول والمقبول وجب وجودهما فلا فرق بينهما في الوجوب والإمكان وأيضا نسبة القابل إلى المقبول بالإمكان العام وهو لا ينافي الوجوب و(بعد التسليم) بناء على أن الفاعل وحده قد يكون في بعض الصور مستقلا موجبا لمفعوله كما مر ولا يتصور ذلك في القابل إذ لا بد له من الفاعل","english":"—a simple thing’s having two effects, as just explained. “It may also be argued” that an agent cannot be receptive “because the agent’s relation to its effect is necessity, whereas the recipient’s relation to what it receives is possibility.” Receiving and producing the same thing would therefore conflict, since their consequences—necessity and possibility—cannot coincide. “The reply is” that when the agent is taken alone, the effect’s existence need not follow, just as the received thing need not exist with the recipient alone. When each is taken together with everything upon which the effect or received thing depends, its existence is necessary. There is therefore no difference in necessity and possibility. Moreover, the recipient’s relation to what it receives is possibility in the general sense, which does not conflict with necessity. “Even after conceding” the argument’s premise—on the ground that an agent alone may sometimes independently necessitate its effect, as previously explained, whereas a recipient cannot do so, since it still needs an agent—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I150.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I149.upper.02","join_out":"I151.upper.01","readings":[{"locus":"كان القابل وحده","status":"A conjunction or comparative particle before كان is unclear; the agent–recipient comparison is explicit in the surrounding text."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I151.lower.01","volume":1,"page":151,"physical_pdf_page":164,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله أحد نوعيه الخ) هذا حاصل ما ذكره في شرح المقاصد من أنه لو فرضنا الإمكان العام فليس معناه أحد نوعيه أي الوجوب بل معناه مفهومه الأعم بحيث يشمل الإمكان الخاص فينافي تعين الوجوب الذي لا يشمله انتهى (أقول) فيه أن منافاة مفهوم الإمكان لمفهوم الوجوب مسلم لكن لا يقدح فيما نحن بصدده من عمومه للوجوب وعدم منافاته له بحسب الصدق فإن معنى كون نسبة القابل إلى المقبول بالإمكان العام هو أنها تصدق تارة بالوجوب كما إذا اعتبرت من حيث الفاعلية وتارة بالإمكان الخاص كما إذا اعتبرت من حيث القابلية فافهم","english":"On “One of its kinds, etc.” This summarises Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid: even assuming general possibility, its meaning is not one of its kinds—necessity—but the broader concept including specific possibility. It therefore conflicts with the restriction to necessity, which does not include specific possibility. End. I say: the concepts of possibility and necessity differ, admittedly, but that does not undermine the point at issue: general possibility includes necessity in its extension and is compatible with it in application. Saying that the recipient’s relation to what it receives is general possibility means that it applies sometimes as necessity, when considered under agency, and sometimes as specific possibility, when considered under receptivity. Understand this.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I151.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I151.lower.02","volume":1,"page":151,"physical_pdf_page":164,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله بجهتين) سواء كانتا لشيء واحد كما نحن فيه أو لشيئين كما قالوا فيما إذا كان قابلا لشيء وفاعلا لشيء آخر من أن فاعليته لذاته وقابليته باعتبار تأثره عما أوجد المقبول","english":"On “Under two aspects”: whether they concern one thing, as here, or two things—as when something receives one thing and produces another. They say its agency belongs to its essence, while its receptivity concerns its being affected by what produces the received thing.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I151.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I151.lower.03","volume":1,"page":151,"physical_pdf_page":164,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله بمعنى لا تناهيها مدة وعدة) لم يتعرض للشدة لأنا متفقون مع الفلاسفة في امتناع اللاتناهي بحسب الشدة كما سيشير إليه الشارح (قوله بمعنى أن القوى الجسمانية الخ) وتفصيل ذلك هو أن الفلاسفة يثبتون للقوى الجسمانية تأثيرا ويشترطون فيه الوضع قطعا منهم بأن النار مثلا لا تسخن كل شيء والشمس لا تضيء بها كل شيء بل ما له بالنسبة إليها وضع مخصوص وقطعوا بأنه يلزم تناهيها بحسب الشدة والمدة والعدة بأن تكون مدة آثارها","english":"On “Without limit in duration and number”: intensity is not mentioned, because we agree with the philosophers that infinite intensity is impossible, as the commentator will indicate. On “No bodily power can, etc.” To explain: the philosophers ascribe efficacy to bodily powers, requiring an appropriate positional relation. They hold, for example, that fire does not heat everything, nor the sun illuminate everything, but only what stands in a particular position relative to them. They maintain that these powers must be finite in intensity, duration and number, their effects having a duration—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I151.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I152.lower.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I151.paratext.01","volume":1,"page":151,"physical_pdf_page":164,"layer":"paratext","attribution":"Printed section heading","arabic":"فصل","english":"Section","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I151.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I151.upper.01","volume":1,"page":151,"physical_pdf_page":164,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"وأنه ليس معنى الإمكان العام أحد نوعيه أعني الوجوب بل الأعم بحيث يشمل الإمكان الخاص ولأنه يمكن عدم المقبول من حيث إنه مقبول رد (بأنه لا امتناع في اجتماع الوجوب واللاوجوب بجهتين) لجواز كون الشيء واجبا لشيء من حيث كونه فاعلا له وغير واجب من حيث كونه قابلا له","english":"—and that general possibility does not mean just one of its kinds, necessity, but the broader notion which includes specific possibility; and that what is received may be absent insofar as it is received—the answer remains that “necessity and non-necessity may coexist under two different aspects.” A thing may be necessary relative to another insofar as the latter acts upon it, but not necessary insofar as the latter receives it.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I151.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I150.upper.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I151.upper.02","volume":1,"page":151,"physical_pdf_page":164,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"فيما يبنى على استناد الممكنات إلى الله تعالى ابتداء (يجوز دوام أفعال القوى الجسمانية) الحالة في الجسم بمعنى لا تناهيها مدة وعدة فإن نعيم أهل الجنة وعذاب أهل النار دائمان أبدا وليس ذلك إلا دوام القوى الجسمانية وكونها فاعلة فعلا غير متناه زمانا ولا عددا (بخلق الله تعالى) وعند الفلاسفة يلزم تناهيها بحسب الشدة بمعنى أن القوى الجسمانية لا تقوى أن","english":"On a consequence of contingent things’ depending directly upon God. “The acts of bodily powers” inhering in bodies “may continue perpetually,” without limit in duration and number. The bliss of the people of Paradise and the punishment of the people of Hell continue forever; this involves bodily powers and their acts continuing without temporal or numerical limit, “through God’s creation.” The philosophers, however, require bodily powers to be finite in intensity: no bodily power can—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I151.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I152.upper.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I152.lower.01","volume":1,"page":152,"physical_pdf_page":165,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"متناهيا ولذا زمانها في جانبي الانتقاص والازدياد بأن لا ينتقص إلى غير نهاية وهو التناهي بحسب الشدة ولا يزداد إلى غير النهاية وهو التناهي بحسب المدة وبهذه الاعتبارات تصير القوى أصنافا الأول قوى يفرض صدور أعمال متوالية عنها مختلفة العدة كرماة تختلف عدد رميهم ولا محالة تكون التي يصدر منها عددا أكثر أقوى من التي يصدر منها عدد أقل ويلزم من ذلك أن يكون عمل غير المتناهية منها غير متناهية عددا والثاني قوى يفرض صدور عمل منها في أزمنة مختلفة كرماة تقطع سهامهم مسافة محدودة في أزمنة مختلفة ولا محالة يكون التي زمانها أقل أشد قوة من التي زمانها أكثر ويلزم من ذلك أن يقع عمل غير المتناهية منها لا في زمان والثالث قوى يفرض صدور عمل منها على الاتصال في أزمنة مختلفة كرماة تختلف أزمنة حركات سهامهم في الهواء ولا محالة تكون التي زمانها أكثر أقوى من التي زمانها أقل ويجب من ذلك أن يقع عمل غير المتناهية منها في زمان غير متناه فالاختلاف الأول بالعدة والثاني بالشدة والثالث بالمدة","english":"—which is finite; likewise the time is bounded in both diminution and increase. It cannot diminish without limit: this is finitude in intensity. It cannot increase without limit: this is finitude in duration. Powers are accordingly considered in three classes. First, powers producing successive acts of different numbers, like archers differing in the number of their shots: the power producing more is stronger, so an infinite power would produce infinitely many acts. Second, powers producing an act in different times, like arrows traversing a specified distance in different durations: the one taking less time is more intense, so an infinitely intense power would act in no time. Third, powers producing continuous acts of different durations, like arrows remaining in motion through the air for different lengths of time: the one acting longer is stronger, so an infinite power would act for an infinite time. The first difference concerns number, the second intensity, the third duration.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I152.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I151.lower.03","readings":[{"locus":"متناهيا ولذا زمانها","status":"The opening conjunction is insecure; the subsequent threefold distinction is fully represented."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I152.lower.02","volume":1,"page":152,"physical_pdf_page":165,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله أسرع منها) أي لا تقوى أن تفعل في زمان (قوله لا تقوى أن تفعل في زمان غير متناه) لم يتعرض هنا للتناهي بحسب العدة وهو أن لا تقوى على مدد غير متناه لأن اللاتناهي بحسب العدة يستلزم اللاتناهي بحسب المدة وإبطال اللازم يستلزم إبطال الملزوم فاقتصر عليه وفي بعض النسخ التعرض له أيضا وهو أظهر","english":"On “Faster than it”: that is, it cannot act [in no time]. On “It cannot act for an infinite time”: numerical finitude is not separately treated here—the inability to act through infinitely many periods—because infinite number entails infinite duration, and refuting the consequence refutes what entails it. Some copies mention it separately as well, which is clearer.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I152.jpg","stage":69,"readings":[{"locus":"أي لا تقوى أن تفعل في زمان","status":"The printed short explanation seems defective: the contextual meaning is “in no time.” The bracketed English makes this explicit rather than passing it off as an intact source clause."}],"editorial":"The remark about other copies is a report in the printed marginal text, not a new collation performed here.","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I152.upper.01","volume":1,"page":152,"physical_pdf_page":165,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"تفعل حركة لا تكون حركة أخرى أسرع منها (والمدة) بمعنى أنها لا تقوى أن تفعل في زمان غير متناه (والعدة) أي لا تقوى على فعل عدده غير متناه (لأن القسري) أي تأثير القوى الحالة في الجسم تأثيرا قسريا وهو التأثير في جسم آخر غير محلها (يختلف باختلاف القابل) المقهور لأنه كلما كان القابل أكبر كان تحريكه بالقاسر أضعف لكون مقاومته ومعاوقته أكثر وأقوى (والطبيعي) أي تأثير القوى الجسمانية تأثيرا طبيعيا وهو التأثير في جسم هو محلها فاندرج فيه التحريك الإرادي الصادر عن النفس الحيوانية يختلف (باختلاف الفاعل) لأنه كلما كان الجسم أعظم مقدارا كانت الطبيعة فيه أقوى وأكثر آثارا","english":"—produce a motion than which no faster motion is possible. “And duration”: it cannot act for an infinite time. “And number”: it cannot produce infinitely many acts. This is “because forced motion”—the efficacy of a bodily power upon a body other than its own subject—“varies with the recipient” subjected to it. The larger the recipient, the weaker the motion imparted by the constraining agent, because resistance and opposition are greater. “Natural motion”—a bodily power’s efficacy within the body that is its own subject, including voluntary motion proceeding from an animal soul—“varies with the agent.” The larger the body, the stronger its nature and the more numerous its effects.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I152.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I151.upper.02","join_out":"I153.upper.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I153.lower.01","volume":1,"page":153,"physical_pdf_page":166,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله ويلزم منه انتهاء حركة الخ) وقد نوقض هذا الدليل إجمالا بالحركات الفلكية فإنها مع عدم تناهيها عندهم مستندة إلى قوى جسمانية لها إدراكات جزئية وتفصيلا بأنه لا يجوز أن تكون تلك القوى أزلية لا تكون لحركاتها مبدأ وأيضا لم لا يجوز أن يكون التفاوت الذي لا بد منه هو التفاوت بالسرعة والبطء وأن تكون حركة الأصغر القسرية أسرع وفي الطبيعية أبطأ من غير انقطاع على أنا لا نسلم أن التفاوت بالزيادة والنقصان يوجب الانقطاع وأجيب بأن حركات الأفلاك إرادية مستندة إلى إرادات","english":"On “The smaller’s motion must then end, etc.” The argument has been countered generally by celestial motions, which they hold to be endless yet dependent upon bodily powers with particular apprehensions. More specifically, why could those powers not be eternal, their motions having no beginning? And why could the required difference not be one of speed and slowness—the smaller’s forced motion being faster, its natural motion slower—without either ceasing? Moreover, we do not concede that a difference through increase and decrease requires termination. The reply is that celestial motions are voluntary and depend upon volitions—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I153.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I154.lower.01","readings":[{"locus":"بأنه لا يجوز أن تكون تلك القوى أزلية","status":"The source appears to omit an interrogative ما in the objection; the English presents the evidently interrogative argument without silently altering the Arabic."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I153.ruled.01","volume":1,"page":153,"physical_pdf_page":166,"layer":"ruled","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله لكونها متجزئة الخ) وهذا لا يصح على عمومه إلا عند من لا يجعل النفس الحيوانية جوهرا مجردا كالنفس البشرية اه منه","english":"On “Because the powers divide, etc.” This is universally valid only for someone who does not regard the animal soul as an immaterial substance like the human soul. End. “From him.”","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I153.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I153.upper.01","volume":1,"page":153,"physical_pdf_page":166,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"لأن القوى الجسمانية المتناسبة إنما تختلف باختلاف محالها بالصغر والكبر لكونها متجزئة بتجزئها (فإذا فرض في حركتيهما) أي الصغيرة والكبيرة المفهومين من اختلاف القابل والفاعل (الاتحاد في المبدأ تفاوت الجانب الآخر) وهو جانب الانتهاء فيكون حركة القابل الصغير أكثر من حركة الكبير لكون المعاوقة فيه أقل وحركة الكبير بالطبع أكثر من حركة الصغير لكون الطبيعة في الكبير أقوى فلزم التناهي بالمدة والعدة لأنه يلزم ضرورة انتهاء حركة القابل الأكبر قبل الأصغر ويلزم منه انتهاء حركة الأصغر لأنها إنما تزيد على حركة الأكبر بمقدار زيادة مقداره وتنقطع حركة الصغير بالطبع قبل الكبير ضرورة أن الجزء لا يقوى على ما يقوى عليه الكل ويلزم منه انتهاء حركة الكبير لكونها على نسبة جسميهما","english":"Corresponding bodily powers differ as their subjects differ in size, because the powers divide with their subjects. “If the two motions”—of the smaller and larger, understood from the difference in recipient or agent—“are supposed to begin together, their other end differs”: the end at which they terminate. The smaller recipient’s motion exceeds the larger’s because it offers less resistance; the larger body’s natural motion exceeds the smaller’s because its nature is stronger. Finitude in duration and number follows. The larger recipient’s motion must end before the smaller’s; the smaller’s must also end, since it exceeds the larger’s motion only by an amount corresponding to the difference in their magnitudes. In natural motion, the smaller’s motion ends before the larger’s, because a part cannot accomplish what the whole accomplishes; the larger’s motion must then also end, since the motions are proportionate to the two bodies.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I153.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I152.upper.final","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I153.upper.02","volume":1,"page":153,"physical_pdf_page":166,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"وههنا حل عقدة قال في شرح المقاصد لما كان امتناع اللاتناهي بحسب الشدة وهو أن يقع الأثر في الزمان الذي هو في غاية القصر بل في الآن ظاهرا لامتناع أن يقع الحركة إلا في زمان قابل للانقسام بحيث يكون القوة التي توقع الحركة في نصف ذلك أشد تأثيرا اقتصروا على بيان امتناع اللاتناهي بحسب المدة والعدة (ورد بعد","english":"Here a difficulty must be untied. Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid says: infinite intensity—an effect occurring in the shortest conceivable time, indeed in an instant—is evidently impossible, since motion occurs only in a divisible time, and a power producing that motion in half the time would be more intense in its effect. They therefore confined their demonstration to the impossibility of infinity in duration and number. “The argument is answered, after—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I153.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I154.upper.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I154.lower.01","volume":1,"page":154,"physical_pdf_page":167,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"وتعقلات جزئية مستندة إلى نفوسها المجردة في ذواتها المفارقة في أفعالها المدركة للجزئيات بواسطة الآلات وكلامنا في تأثير القوى الحالة في الأجسام وبأن كلامنا المفهوم في الاختلاف والتفاوت بحسب الزمان والعدد وبمعنى الزيادة والنقصان وزمان الحركة وعددها لا في التفاوت بحسب السرعة والبطء وسيأتي في باب التسلسل ما يفيد أن التفاوت بالزيادة والنقصان فيما بين خواص المتناهي فليتأمل","english":"—and particular cognitions dependent upon their souls, immaterial in themselves and separate in their acts, apprehending particulars by means of instruments. Our discussion concerns the efficacy of powers inhering in bodies. It also concerns differences in time and number—increases and decreases in the duration and number of motions—not differences in speed and slowness. The discussion of infinite regress will give grounds for treating differences through increase and decrease as properties of the finite. Let this be considered.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I154.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I153.lower.01","readings":[{"locus":"المفارقة في أفعالها / وبأن كلامنا المفهوم / فيما بين خواص","status":"Several brief expressions are insecure. The account is retained as a reported answer, including its own distinction between souls and bodily powers."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I154.lower.02","volume":1,"page":154,"physical_pdf_page":167,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله فإن الحوادث مستندة عندنا الخ) ولا نشترط في ظهور الآثار المترتبة على القوى تعلق الله تعالى وضعا مخصوصا فلا مانع عندنا في دوام تلك الآثار والأفعال","english":"On “For on our account events depend upon God, etc.” We do not impose a particular configuration as a condition upon God’s attachment [of power] for the appearance of effects associated with those powers. There is therefore no obstacle, on our account, to those effects and acts continuing.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I154.jpg","stage":69,"readings":[{"locus":"تعلق الله تعالى وضعا مخصوصا","status":"The syntactic relation around تعلق is unclear; the English is provisional rather than a claim to an emended text."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I154.lower.03","volume":1,"page":154,"physical_pdf_page":167,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله فيكون الشيء متقدما على نفسه الخ) أورد عليه أنه إن أريد التقدم بالزمان فغير لازم لأن","english":"On “The thing would thus precede itself, etc.” The objection is that, if temporal priority is intended, it does not follow, because—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I154.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I155.lower.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I154.paratext.01","volume":1,"page":154,"physical_pdf_page":167,"layer":"paratext","attribution":"Printed section heading","arabic":"فصل","english":"Section","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I154.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I154.upper.01","volume":1,"page":154,"physical_pdf_page":167,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"تسليم التأثير) للقوى فإن الحوادث عندنا مستندة إلى الله تعالى (بأنه إنما يتم لو كانت القوة مقدار الجسم) حتى لو كان مقدار الصغير نصف مقدار الكبير كانت قوته ومعاوقته وتحريكه نصف قوة ومعاوقة الكبير وتحريكه وهو ممنوع لجواز أن تكون القوة من الأعراض التي لا تنقسم بانقسام المحل كالوحدة والأبوة","english":"—granting efficacy” to those powers (for on our account events depend upon God), “by saying that it succeeds only if the power is proportionate to the body.” The smaller body’s being half the magnitude of the larger would have to make its power, resistance and motion half the larger’s power, resistance and motion. This is denied: the power might be one of those accidents which do not divide when their subject divides, such as unity and fatherhood.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I154.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I153.upper.02","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I154.upper.02","volume":1,"page":154,"physical_pdf_page":167,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"في الدور والتسلسل أوردهما في هذا الباب لورودهما على العلية والمعلولية (يستحيل الدور) قدمه على إبطال التسلسل لتوقفه عليه (وهو) حقيقته كما قال بعض المحققين توقف كل واحد من الشيئين على الآخر ويلزمه (توقف الشيء على ما يتوقف عليه) فهو تعريف باللازم اختاره لأنه أظهر واستلزامه لتقدم الشيء على نفسه وأما كان مستحيلا (لامتناع) ما يستلزمه أعني (تقدم الشيء على نفسه ضروري) ووجه الاستلزام أن الشيء إذا كان علة للآخر كان متقدما عليه وإذا كان الآخر علة له كان متقدما والتقدم على المتقدم على الشيء متقدم على ذات الشيء فيكون الشيء متقدما على نفسه ويلزمه كونه متأخرا","english":"On circularity and infinite regress. These are included here because they concern being a cause and being an effect. “A causal circle is impossible.” It is refuted before an infinite regress because the latter’s refutation depends upon it. “It is”—in its reality, as some investigators explain—each of two things depending upon the other. This entails “a thing’s depending upon what depends upon it.” The definition is through a necessary concomitant, chosen because it is clearer and entails a thing’s being prior to itself. It is impossible because “the impossibility of a thing’s being prior to itself is self-evident.” The entailment is as follows: if one thing causes another, it precedes it; if the other causes the first, it precedes the first. What precedes what precedes a thing precedes that thing. The thing would thus precede itself, and consequently be posterior—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I154.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I155.upper.01","readings":[{"locus":"وأما كان مستحيلا / وإذا كان الآخر علة له كان متقدما","status":"One transition and a pronoun in the printed explanation are compressed; the cause–priority inference is preserved without supplying a new proof."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I155.lower.01","volume":1,"page":155,"physical_pdf_page":168,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"الكلام في العلية وإن أريد بالعلية فنفس المدعى لأن قولنا الشيء لا يتقدم على نفسه بالعلية بمنزلة قولنا الشيء لا يكون علة لنفسه أجيب بأن المراد هو التقدم بالمعنى الذي يصح قولنا وجد فوجد على ما هو اللازم من كون الشيء علة لشيء بمعنى أنه ما لم يوجد ذلك لم يوجد هذا ولا خفاء في استحالته بالنظر إلى الشيء ونفسه كذا في شرح المقاصد وأقول حاصل الجواب هو اختيار الثاني من الترديدين ومنع أنه نفس المدعى بل هو لازمها فتدبر","english":"—the discussion concerns causality. If causal priority is intended, the claim simply repeats what is to be proved: “a thing cannot be causally prior to itself” amounts to “a thing cannot cause itself.” The reply takes priority in the sense in which one may correctly say “it existed, and therefore the other existed,” as required by one thing’s causing another: unless the first exists, the second does not exist. This is plainly impossible when applied to a thing and itself. Such is the answer in Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid. I say: the answer chooses the second alternative, denying that it merely repeats the thesis; rather, it gives a necessary consequence of it. Reflect.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I155.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I154.lower.04","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I155.lower.02","volume":1,"page":155,"physical_pdf_page":168,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله والكل بديهي الاستحالة الخ) وربما يبين ذلك بأن التقدم والتأخر والاحتياج نسب لا تعقل إلا بين شيئين وبأن نسبة المحتاج إليه إلى المحتاج بالوجوب ونسبة المحتاج إلى المحتاج إليه بالإمكان فلا يجتمعان والكل ضعيف فتفطن","english":"On “All this is self-evidently impossible, etc.” Sometimes this is explained by saying that priority, posteriority and need are relations conceivable only between two things; or that what is needed stands to the dependent through necessity, while the dependent stands to what it needs through possibility, so the two cannot coincide. All of these explanations are weak. Attend carefully.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I155.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I155.lower.03","volume":1,"page":155,"physical_pdf_page":168,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله وليس ذلك مطلق التسلسل الخ) قال في حاشية منقولة منه هذا وهو ترتب أمور غير متناهية سواء كان في العلية والمعلولية أو في الوضع انتهى (أقول) بل مطلق التسلسل هو وجود أمور غير متناهية سواء كانت مجتمعة في الوجود أولا وسواء كانت مترتبة أولا وقد فصلناه في رسالتنا الجديدة وبينا فيها مذهب المتكلمين والفلاسفة وحققنا على رأينا ما هو الجائز منه وما هو الممتنع فليراجع","english":"On “This is not regress in its most general sense, etc.” A gloss transmitted from him defines it as an ordering of infinitely many things, whether in causality or in position. End. I say: regress in the unrestricted sense is rather the existence of infinitely many things, whether or not they coexist, and whether or not they are ordered. We have explained this in our new treatise, set out the positions of theologians and philosophers, and established what, in our view, is possible and impossible. Consult it.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I155.jpg","stage":69,"editorial":"The first-person reference to a new treatise is the printed annotator’s statement; no author or title is inferred.","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I155.lower.04","volume":1,"page":155,"physical_pdf_page":168,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله ولأن بعض ما ذكر الخ) كبرهان التكافؤ (قوله ولأنه المطلوب الخ) وذلك لأن المطلوب المحقق","english":"On “Because some arguments, etc.”: such as the proof from the correspondence of correlatives. On “Because it is what is sought, etc.”: for the actual objective—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I155.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I156.lower.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I155.ruled.01","volume":1,"page":155,"physical_pdf_page":168,"layer":"ruled","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله مطلق التسلسل الخ) وهو ترتب أمور غير متناهية سواء في العلية والمعلولية أو في الوضع اه منه","english":"On “Regress in its most general sense, etc.” It is an ordering of infinitely many things, whether in causality or in position. End. “From him.”","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I155.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I155.upper.01","volume":1,"page":155,"physical_pdf_page":168,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"عن نفسه والكل بديهي الاستحالة (و) يستحيل (التسلسل وهو ترتب غير متناه في العلية والمعلولية إلى لا نهاية) بأن يكون كل ما هو معروض للعلية معروضا للمعلولية ولا ينتهي إلى ما يعرض له العلية دون المعلولية وليس ذلك مطلق التسلسل لكنه اختير لأن بعض ما ذكر في بيان الاستحالة إنما ينطبق عليه ولأنه المطلوب ههنا استحالته وإنما يستحيل (لأن) العلل والمعلولات","english":"—to itself. All this is self-evidently impossible. “And an infinite regress is impossible: an unending ordering in causehood and effecthood,” in which everything characterised as cause is also characterised as effect, never reaching something which is a cause but not an effect. This is not regress in its most general sense. It is chosen because some arguments for impossibility apply only to it, and because its impossibility is what is sought here. It is impossible “because,” if causes and effects—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I155.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I154.upper.02","join_out":"I156.upper.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I156.lower.01","volume":1,"page":156,"physical_pdf_page":169,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"في الكلام إثبات الواجب تعالى وهو موقوف على بطلان هذا النوع من التسلسل فليتأمل (قوله موجود ممكن الخ) أما وجودها فلانحصار أجزائها في الموجود ومعلوم أن المركب لا ينعدم إلا بانعدام كل أجزائه أو شيء منها فإن قيل طالما المركب من الأجزاء الموجودة قد يكون اعتباريا لا تحقق له في الخارج كالمركب من الإنسان والحجر أجيب بأن المراد من ذلك هو أنه ليس موجودا واحدا يقوم به وجود غير وجودات الأجزاء وإلا فقد صرحوا بأن المركب في الخارج قد يكون من حقيقة الآحاد الخارجية كالعشر من الرجال هذا إذا أريد معروض الهيئة الاجتماعية أما إذا أريد مجموع العارض والمعروض فليس مما نحن فيه لأنه معدوم وأما إمكانها فلافتقارها إلى جزئها الممكن ومعلوم أن المفتقر إلى الممكن أولى بالإمكان","english":"—of this discussion is to establish the Necessary Existent, exalted is He, and this depends upon refuting this kind of regress. Consider this. On “A contingent existent, etc.” The aggregate exists because its parts are all existent. A composite becomes nonexistent only if all or some of its parts are nonexistent. If it is objected that a composite of existent parts may be merely conceptual, without extramental actuality, as a composite of a human and a stone, the reply is that this means it is not one existent bearing an existence different from those of its parts. They explicitly allow an external composite constituted by the realities of external individuals, such as ten men. This concerns the subject of the configuration of aggregation. If the aggregate of that configuration and its subject is meant, that is not the present case, for it is nonexistent. Its contingency follows from its need for a contingent part: what needs a contingent is all the more contingent.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I156.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I155.lower.04","readings":[{"locus":"فإن قيل طالما المركب","status":"The short word immediately after فإن قيل is insecure; no argument is omitted in English."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I156.lower.02","volume":1,"page":156,"physical_pdf_page":169,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله كانت موجودة قبل وجود نفسها الخ) وذلك لأن الكلام في المؤثر المستقل ولا خفاء في لزوم تقدمه فلا يرد ما يقال","english":"On “It would exist before its own existence, etc.” This is because the discussion concerns an independently sufficient producer, whose priority is evident. The following objection therefore does not apply—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I156.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I157.lower.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I156.ruled.01","volume":1,"page":156,"physical_pdf_page":169,"layer":"ruled","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله وتلك الجملة موجود الخ) أما أنها موجود فلوجود جميع آحادها وأما أنها ممكن فلإمكان تلك الآحاد المأخوذة فيها اه منه","english":"On “That aggregate is a contingent existent, etc.” It exists because all its individuals exist; it is contingent because the individuals included in it are contingent. End. “From him.”","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I156.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I156.ruled.02","volume":1,"page":156,"physical_pdf_page":169,"layer":"ruled","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله ولا جزءا منها الخ) ولا يرد عليه ما أورده المحشي الدواني من أن الذات المستقل فيه الجزء الذي هو ما فوق المعلول الأخير إلى غير نهاية لاشتماله على علل جميع أجزائه أو داخل في هذا المجموع أيضا فلا شيء فوق هذا المجموع يكون علة إلا الواجب وهو المطلوب اه منه","english":"On “Nor a part of it, etc.” The objection made by the glossator al-Dawānī does not apply: that the independently sufficient part is everything above the final effect, without end, since it contains the causes of all its parts. [The printed reply adds that] it is also included in this aggregate, so nothing beyond the aggregate can be its cause except the Necessary Existent, which is what is sought. End. “From him.”","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I156.jpg","stage":69,"readings":[{"locus":"من أن الذات المستقل فيه الجزء / أو داخل","status":"The beginning and transition from al-Dawānī’s objection to the reply are insecure; brackets mark the apparent change of voice rather than silently reconstructing the source."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I156.upper.01","volume":1,"page":156,"physical_pdf_page":169,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"لو تسلسلت من غير أن تنتهي إلى علة محضة لكان هناك جملة هي نفس مجموع الممكنات الموجودة المعلول كل من آحادها للواحد منها وتلك الجملة موجود ممكن و(المؤثر المستقل لتلك الجملة ليس نفسها) وإلا كانت موجودة قبل وجود نفسها ولا يتوهم أن الجملة إنما تطلق على المتناهي إذ المراد ههنا هو تلك الآحاد بحيث لا يخرج عنها واحد منها وهذا الاعتبار معقول في الأمور المتناهية وغير المتناهية (ولا جزءا منها) وإلا أوجد ذلك الجزء نفسه","english":"—regressed without terminating in a sheer cause, there would be an aggregate identical with all the existing contingents, each individual caused by one of the others. That aggregate is a contingent existent. “Its independently sufficient producer is not the aggregate itself,” for otherwise it would exist before its own existence. Do not suppose that “aggregate” applies only to the finite. Here it means those individuals considered so that not one of them is excluded, a consideration intelligible for finite and infinite things alike. “Nor is its producer a part of it,” for that part would then bring itself into existence—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I156.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I155.upper.01","join_out":"I157.upper.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I157.lower.01","volume":1,"page":157,"physical_pdf_page":170,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"من أنا لا نسلم استحالة كون الجملة علة تامة لنفسها إنما يستحيل لو لزم تقدمها وهو ممنوع لما سبق أن العلة التامة للمركب تمتنع تقدمها بتمامها فضلا عن أن يجب","english":"—that we do not concede the impossibility of an aggregate’s being its own complete cause. This would be impossible only if the aggregate had to precede itself, which is denied: as explained earlier, a composite’s complete cause cannot precede it in its entirety, much less must it do so.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I157.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I156.lower.final","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I157.lower.02","volume":1,"page":157,"physical_pdf_page":170,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله لأن موجد الكل موجد لأجزائه) قال في الحاشية لأن الكلام في الموجد المستقل انتهى أي ولا خفاء في لزوم كون الموجد المستقل للمركب فاعلا في كل جزء منه فاندفع ما يقال إنا لا نسلم استحالة كون العلة الفاعلة للسلسلة بعض أجزائها إنما يستحيل لو لزم كونها علة لكل جزء من أجزائها حتى نفسه وهو ممنوع لجواز أن تكون بعض أجزاء المركب مستندا إلى غير فاعله كالخشب من السرير فإنها مستندة إلى غير النجار فعلته المستقلة ليست هي النجار وحده بل هو مع فاعل الخشبات","english":"On “What produces the whole produces its parts”: the gloss explains that the discussion concerns an independently sufficient producer. End. Such a producer of a composite must act upon every part. This answers the proposed objection that part of a series might be its efficient cause: this would be impossible only if it caused every part, including itself; but some parts of a composite might derive from another agent, as a bed’s wood derives from something other than the carpenter. The independently sufficient cause is therefore not the carpenter alone, but the carpenter together with the producer of the wood.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I157.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I157.lower.03","volume":1,"page":157,"physical_pdf_page":170,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"لكن بقي أنه إن أريد بكون العلة المستقلة علة لكل جزء أنها بنفسها علة مستقلة حتى تكون علة هذا الجزء هي بعينها علة ذلك الجزء فممنوع ضرورة أن المركب قد يكون بحيث يحدث أجزاؤه شيئا فشيئا فعند حدوث الجزء الأول إن لم توجد العلة المستقلة المفروضة لزم تقدم المعلول على علته وإن وجدت لزم تخلف المعلولات الأخرى عنها والكل باطل وإن أريد أنها علة لكل جزء بنفسها أو بجزئها من غير افتقار إلى أمر خارج بحيث إذا كان المعلول مرتب الأجزاء كانت علته أيضا كذلك فيرد أنه يجوز أن يكون من أجزاء السلسلة ما هو علة لها بهذا المعنى وهو مجموع الأجزاء التي لا يخرج منها إلا المعلول المحض المتأخر عن الكل من غير أن يلزم علية الشيء لنفسه كذا حققه بعضهم","english":"But a difficulty remains. If “the independently sufficient cause causes every part” means that it is itself the independently sufficient cause of each, so that precisely the same cause produces this part and that part, the claim is denied. A composite’s parts may occur successively. When the first occurs, if the supposed independently sufficient cause does not yet exist, an effect precedes its cause; if it does exist, the other effects fail to follow it. Both are false. Alternatively, the statement may mean that the cause produces each part through itself or a part of itself, without needing anything external; if the effect’s parts are ordered, its cause is ordered likewise. It may then be objected that part of the series could cause the whole in this sense: the aggregate of all the parts except the sheer effect, posterior to all of them. This would not entail a thing’s causing itself. Thus some have established the objection.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I157.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I157.ruled.01","volume":1,"page":157,"physical_pdf_page":170,"layer":"ruled","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله والمراد بالدور الخ) فلا يرد أن هذا دليل على إثبات الواجب من غير احتياج إلى إبطال الدور اه منه","english":"On “Here ‘circle’ means, etc.” This avoids the objection that the argument establishes the Necessary Existent without needing first to refute a causal circle. End. “From him.”","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I157.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I157.upper.01","volume":1,"page":157,"physical_pdf_page":170,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"لأن موجد الكل موجد لأجزائه ومن جملتها ذلك الجزء وللمحال (للدور) المستحيل والمراد بالدور هنا لازمه (بل) المؤثر المستقل بها يكون (خارجا) فيكون (واجبا) بالذات لأن الموجد الخارج عن سلسلة الممكنات لا يكون إلا واجبا","english":"—because what brings the whole into existence brings its parts into existence, including that very part. This would entail the impossible “circle”; here “circle” means what follows from a circle. “Rather,” the independently sufficient cause of the aggregate must be “external” to it, and therefore “necessary” in itself: a producer outside the series of all contingent things can only be necessary.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I157.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I156.upper.final","join_out":"I158.upper.01","readings":[{"locus":"ومن جملتها ذلك الجزء وللمحال","status":"The connective immediately before the lemma للدور is unclear; the circular consequence is stated without supplying missing source wording."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I158.lower.01","volume":1,"page":158,"physical_pdf_page":171,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله لانحصار الموجود في الواجب والممكن الخ) إشارة إلى دفع ما يقال من أنا لا نسلم أن الخارج من السلسلة يكون واجبا لجواز أن توجد سلاسل غير متناهية كل منها تستند إلى علة خارجة منها داخلة في سلسلة أخرى من غير انتهاء إلى الواجب فليتأمل ثم في شرح المقاصد ما حاصله أنه إن قيل الدليل منقوض بالجملة التي هي عبارة عن الواجب وجميع الممكنات فإن علتها ليست نفسها ولا جزءا منها لما ذكر ولا خارجا لاستلزامه مع تعدد الواجب معلوليته واجتماع المؤثرين على معلول إن كان علة لكل جزء وأحدهما ما إن كان علة لبعض الأجزاء أجيب بأن الفاعل المستقل فيما نحن فيه لا يمكن أن يكون جزءا من الجملة للزوم كونه علة لنفسه تحقيقا لمعنى الاستقلال بخلاف المركب من الواجب والممكنات فإنه يجوز أن يستقل بإيجاد بعض أجزائه الذي هو موجود بذاته مستغن عن غيره انتهى","english":"On “Since being is restricted to the necessary and contingent, etc.” This answers the objection that an external cause need not be necessary: there might be infinitely many series, each dependent upon a cause external to it but included in another, without termination in the Necessary Existent. Consider this. Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid then raises, in substance, a counterexample: the aggregate consisting of the Necessary Existent and all contingents. Its cause cannot be itself or a part, for the reasons given; nor can it be external, since this would entail another necessary being, the first Necessary Existent’s being caused, and two producers of one effect if it causes every part—or one of these consequences if it causes only some. The answer is that an independently sufficient agent in the case at issue cannot be part of the aggregate, since independence would require it to cause itself. But the aggregate of the Necessary Existent and contingents differs: the member which exists through itself and needs nothing else may independently produce it. End.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I158.jpg","stage":69,"readings":[{"locus":"وأحدهما ما إن كان علة لبعض الأجزاء / يستقل بإيجاد بعض أجزائه الذي","status":"The short qualification and pronoun arrangement are compressed or insecure. The reported response concerns the self-existent member acting as cause; no independent emendation is asserted."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I158.lower.02","volume":1,"page":158,"physical_pdf_page":171,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(أقول) إن أريد من استقلاله بإيجاده كل جزء من أجزائه استلزم علية الشيء لنفسه أيضا ومعلولية الواجب وإن أريد استقلاله بإيجاده غير الأجزاء فيرد عليه مع أنه يناقض ما سبق من لزوم كون العلة المستقلة للمركب علة لكل جزء منه أنه لم لا يجوز ذلك فيما نحن فيه فتدبر ثم قال (والدليل) منع آخر وهو أنا لا نسلم افتقار السلسلة المفروضة إلى علة غير علل الآحاد وإنما يلزم لو كان لها وجود مغاير لوجودات الآحاد المعلل كل منها بالآخر وقولكم إنها ممكن بمجرد عبارة بل هي ممكنات تحقق كل منها بعلته من أين يلزم الافتقار إلى علة أخرى وما يقال إن وجودات الآحاد غير وجود كل منها كلام خال من التحصيل انتهى","english":"I say: if independence means producing every part, the same consequence follows—a thing causes itself and the Necessary Existent is caused. If it means independently producing something other than the parts, then, besides contradicting the earlier assertion that a composite’s independent cause causes every part, why could the same not be allowed in the present case? Reflect. He then gives another objection to the proof: we do not concede that the supposed series needs a cause other than the causes of its individual members. That follows only if the series has an existence different from the existences of the individuals, each caused by another. Saying that “it is contingent” is a mere expression: there are many contingents, each actualised through its own cause. Why must another cause be needed? The statement that the existences of the individuals differ from the existence of each individual is empty of substance. End.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I158.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I158.lower.03","volume":1,"page":158,"physical_pdf_page":171,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"أقول إن أريد أن وجودات الآحاد غير وجود كل منها على الانفراد فهو كلام صحيح في غاية التحصيل ضرورة صحة أن يقال هذه الدار تسع كلا من الآحاد ولا تسع جميعها وإن","english":"I say: if the claim is that the individuals’ collective existence differs from the existence of each taken separately, it is perfectly sound and meaningful. One can plainly say that this house accommodates each individual but does not accommodate them all. If, however—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I158.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I159.lower.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I158.ruled.01","volume":1,"page":158,"physical_pdf_page":171,"layer":"ruled","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله أي جزءا الخ) وهذا لا ينافي استقلاله وقد فرض أنه واقع به لا بغيره ليمتنع الدور اه منه","english":"On “Meaning a part, etc.” This does not conflict with its independence. The part is supposed to be produced by this cause, not another, so that the circle is excluded. End. “From him.”","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I158.jpg","stage":69,"readings":[{"locus":"ليمـتنع الدور","status":"The short final expression has insecure letters; the apparent reference is to excluding a circle."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I158.upper.01","volume":1,"page":158,"physical_pdf_page":171,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"لانحصار الموجود في الواجب والممكن (يوجب) فيكون لا محالة (شيئا من الجملة) أي جزءا من أجزائها","english":"—since being is restricted to the necessary and the contingent. That external cause “necessarily produces”—and thus inevitably brings into being—“something of the aggregate,” meaning one of its parts.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I158.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I157.upper.01","editorial":"The short upper verb is read as يوجب on the enlarged source; the following unvocalised فيكون is construed causatively (فيكوّن), not as “then becomes.”","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I159.lower.01","volume":1,"page":159,"physical_pdf_page":172,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"أريد أنها غير وجود كلها جميعا فباطل فضلا عن خلوه عن التحصيل فليتأمل ثم اعلم أن الآحاد قد تعتبر فردا فردا من غير اعتبار كونها معروضة للهيئة الاجتماعية فهي بهذا الاعتبار موجودات متعددة لا موجود واحد وقد تعتبر معروضة للهيئة الاجتماعية بحيث تكون الهيئة خارجة وهي بهذا الاعتبار موجود واحد وحدة اعتبارية مركب من الموجودات المتعددة وكلامنا إنما هو في هذا وقد تعتبر بالهيئة الاجتماعية بحيث تكون داخلة في المركب وبهذا الاعتبار مركب معدوم لا موجود ضرورة انعدام جزئه الذي هو الهيئة","english":"—it is claimed that their collective existence differs from the existence of all of them together, the claim is false, not merely empty. Consider this. Know that individuals may be considered one by one, without their being subjects of a configuration of aggregation. They are then many existents, not one. They may instead be considered as subjects of that configuration, with the configuration external to their constitution. They are then one existent by a unity of consideration, composed of many existents. This is the case under discussion. Finally, they may be considered together with the configuration, as though it entered the composite’s constitution. The composite is then nonexistent rather than existent, because its supposed part—the configuration—is nonexistent.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I159.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I158.lower.04","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I159.lower.02","volume":1,"page":159,"physical_pdf_page":172,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"إذا عرفت هذا فالمركب الذي كلامنا فيه إذا كان كل من آحاده معلولا لما قبله إلى غير النهاية فله جهتان إحداهما كون كل من آحاده جزء مادة ذلك المجموع المركب والثانية كون كل منها سوى المعلول الأخير علة فاعلية لما يليه منها ومجموع تلك الآحاد بالجهتين علة تامة لهذا المركب ولا حجر في اتحادهما لما مر أن لزوم تقدم العلة على المعلول إنما هو في العلة الغير التامة وأيضا لا حجر في كون بعض منها علة فاعلية مستقلة له بالمعنى الذي سبق من بعض المحققين من غير لزوم شيء من المحذورات فظهر أن المركب من الواجب وجميع الممكنات كما نحن فيه في جواز كونه علة تامة لنفسه كما أنه فيما نحن فيه مثله في جواز كون مؤثره المستقل جزءا منه بلا فرق","english":"Once this is understood, consider the composite at issue, whose members are each caused by the preceding member without end. It has two aspects: each individual is a material part of the composite aggregate; and each, except the final effect, is an efficient cause of the member following it. The aggregate of the individuals in these two respects is a complete cause of the composite. There is no obstacle to their identity, since, as explained, the requirement that a cause precede its effect applies only to an incomplete cause. Nor is there an obstacle to some of them being its independently sufficient efficient cause in the sense previously stated by certain investigators, without the alleged impossibilities. Thus the aggregate of the Necessary Existent and all contingents resembles the present case in possibly being its own complete cause, while the present case resembles it in possibly having one of its parts as its independent producer.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I159.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I159.lower.03","volume":1,"page":159,"physical_pdf_page":172,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"والتحقيق الذي يندفع به جميع الشبهة هو أن استقلال المؤثر لا يكون إلا بأن يكون وجوده بذاته مستغنيا عن جميع ما سواه إذ لو كان من غيره لم يكن مستقلا في التأثير بل كان بتوسط ذلك الغير ومن المعلومات الجلية امتناع استقلال الممكن في التأثير في الغير بهذا المعنى فثبت أن ذلك المؤثر لا يكون إلا واجبا غنيا عما سواه وهو المطلوب فقط فإنه تحقيق بديع","english":"The investigation which removes all the difficulty is that a producer can be independently sufficient only if its existence is through itself and independent of everything else. If it derived from another, it would not act independently, but through that other. It is evidently impossible for a contingent thing independently to affect another in this sense. The producer must therefore be necessary and independent of all else. That alone is the desired conclusion. It is an admirable clarification.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I159.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I159.lower.04","volume":1,"page":159,"physical_pdf_page":172,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله إذ لو وقع جميع الأجزاء بغير الخ) أقول عدم وقوع جزء من أجزائها بذلك الخارج لا يستلزم وقوع جميعها بغير ذلك الخارج لجواز أن لا يوجد الخارج شيئا منها على الانفراد بل يوجد جميعها فلا يوجب","english":"On “For if all the parts were produced by another, etc.” I say: failure of a part to be produced by that external cause does not entail that the whole is produced by something other than it. The external cause might produce none of them separately, but produce them all together. Thus it does not entail—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I159.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I160.lower.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I159.ruled.01","volume":1,"page":159,"physical_pdf_page":172,"layer":"ruled","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله واقعا بغيره) لأن الاستقلال أعم من أن يكون علة بلا واسطة أو معها اه منه","english":"On “Produced by something other than it”: independent sufficiency is broader than being a cause without an intermediary; it also includes being a cause through an intermediary. End. “From him.”","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I159.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I159.upper.01","volume":1,"page":159,"physical_pdf_page":172,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"إذ لو وقع جميع الأجزاء بغير ذلك الخارج كان المجموع واقعا بغيره","english":"For if all the parts were produced by something other than that external cause, the aggregate would be produced by something other than it.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I159.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I160.upper.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I160.lower.01","volume":1,"page":160,"physical_pdf_page":173,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"الانتهاء إلى الواجب انقطاع السلسلة كما قال في شرح المقاصد ما حاصله إنه وإن سلم لزوم الانتهاء إلى الواجب فلا نسلم لزوم بطلان التسلسل لجواز أن يكون مجموع العلل والمعلولات الغير المتناهية موجودا ممكنا مستندا إلى الواجب انتهى إلا أن يقال وقوع المجموع بالمعنى الذي نحن فيه بالخارج يستلزم وقوع شيء من أجزائه به على الانفراد فإنه وإن سبق أن حكمها بالانفراد قد يغاير حكمها بالاجتماع لكن لا عكس ضرورة أنه كما صدق أن الدار تسع جميع الآحاد صدق أنها تسع كلا منها على الانفراد فتدبر فإنه دقيق","english":"—that terminating in the Necessary Existent brings the series to an end. Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid says, in substance: even granting that it must terminate in the Necessary Existent, it does not follow that the regress is false. The aggregate of infinitely many causes and effects might be a contingent existent dependent upon the Necessary Existent. End. Unless it be said that producing the aggregate in the sense at issue through the external cause entails producing some of its members separately through it. Although, as noted, what holds of individuals separately may differ from what holds of them jointly, the converse does not apply: whenever a house accommodates all the individuals together, it accommodates each separately. Reflect: the point is subtle.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I160.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I159.lower.04","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I160.lower.02","volume":1,"page":160,"physical_pdf_page":173,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله إذ ليس في المجموع شيء سوى الأجزاء الخ) أقول إن أراد قوله فيما سبق وتلك الجملة موجود ممكن أنها موجود سوى الأجزاء فمع أنه ممنوع كما مر ينافي ما ههنا وإن أراد بأنها موجود هو نفس الآحاد كما ههنا فينضم ورود المنع الذي مر من أنا لا نسلم افتقار السلسلة إلى غير علل الآحاد فافهم","english":"On “The aggregate contains nothing beyond its parts, etc.” I say: if the earlier assertion “that aggregate is a contingent existent” means an existent other than the parts, this is not only denied, as above, but conflicts with the present statement. If it means an existent identical with the individuals, as here, the preceding objection applies: we do not concede that the series needs anything other than the causes of the individuals. Understand this.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I160.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I160.lower.03","volume":1,"page":160,"physical_pdf_page":173,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله فلم يكن ذلك الخارج الخ) إن قيل كما سبق أن عدم وقوع شيء من أجزائها بالخارج لا يستلزم وقوع جميعها بغيره كذلك لا يستلزم وقوع شيء منها به وقوع جميعها","english":"On “That external cause would then not be, etc.” If it is objected that, just as no member’s being produced by the external cause does not entail their all being produced by another cause, so one member’s being produced through it does not entail all of them being produced—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I160.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I161.lower.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I160.ruled.01","volume":1,"page":160,"physical_pdf_page":173,"layer":"ruled","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله واجبا بالذات) فلا يجوز أن يكون هو معلولا لجزء آخر منها حتى لا تنقطع السلسلة اه منه","english":"On “Necessary in itself”: it therefore cannot itself be an effect of another member, so that the series would fail to terminate. End. “From him.”","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I160.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I160.upper.01","volume":1,"page":160,"physical_pdf_page":173,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"إذ ليس في المجموع شيء سوى الأجزاء فلم يكن ذلك الخارج علة للمجموع فإذا كان الخارج الواجب موجدا لجزء من أجزاء الجملة (فينقطع) إلى ذلك الجزء سلسلة المعلولات لكون الموجد الخارج عن جميع الممكنات واجبا بالذات وعدم جواز أن يكون ذلك الجزء معلولا لشيء من أجزاء الجملة لامتناع توارد العلتين المستقلتين على واحد لأن الكلام في المؤثر المستقل فيلزم الخلف من وجهين لأن المفروض أن السلسلة غير منقطعة وأن كل جزء منها معلول لجزء آخر منها (ولأنا نفصل من السلسلة جملة ينقص واحد) من طرفها المتناهي فيحصل","english":"—since the aggregate contains nothing beyond its parts. That external cause would then not be a cause of the aggregate. If the external Necessary Existent produces a member of the aggregate, “the series of effects terminates” at that member. The producer external to all contingents is necessary in itself, and that member cannot also be caused by a member of the aggregate: two independently sufficient causes cannot converge upon one effect, and independent sufficiency is what is at issue. A contradiction follows in two respects: the series was supposed not to terminate, and every member was supposed to be caused by another member of it. “And because we separate from the series a series shorter by one,” at its finite end, there result—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I160.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I159.upper.01","join_out":"I161.upper.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I161.lower.01","volume":1,"page":161,"physical_pdf_page":174,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"به مع أنه المطلوب (قلت) لما كان الكلام في المؤثر المستقل فلا جرم في أن ثبوت تأثيره في شيء منها يوجب تأثيره في جميعها تحقيقا لمعنى الاستقلال كما مر فتدبر فإن قيل إذا كان المطلوب إثبات كون الخارج مؤثرا مستقلا في الجميع كفى في البيان أن يقال إذا بطل كون المجموع وكذا كون جزئه علة لذلك المجموع بقي كون الخارج علة له فيكون واجبا موجدا للجميع فتنقطع إليه سلسلة الممكنات من غير افتقار إلى زيادة المقدمة القائلة إيجاد الخارج لشيء من الجملة ثم بيان استلزامه لإيجاد الجميع (قلت) لما كان معنى انقطاع السلسلة كما مر إيجاد الخارج لجزء منها لما سبق من أن الانتهاء إلى الواجب لا يوجب بطلان التسلسل زادوا في الدليل تلك المقدمة دفعا لهذا وإليه أشار «مد ظله» بقوله فتنقطع إلى ذلك الجزء دون أن يقول إلى الواجب فاحفظه فإنه من الدقائق التي لا ينبغي أن يمسك فيها (قوله ثم نطبق الخ) وهذا","english":"—through it, although that is the point to be proved. I reply: since the discussion concerns an independently sufficient producer, establishing its production of one member entails its production of all of them, thereby realising the meaning of independence explained above. Reflect. If it is asked why, to establish the external cause’s independent production of the whole, we do not simply say that the aggregate and its part cannot cause that aggregate, leaving an external cause which is necessary and produces everything, so the contingent series terminates in it—without adding that the external cause produces one member and then explaining why it must produce all—I reply: termination of the series here means the external cause’s producing a member, as previously explained. Merely terminating in the Necessary Existent does not disprove an infinite regress. The additional premise excludes this possibility. Hence his wording—may his protection endure—“it terminates in that member,” rather than “in the Necessary Existent.” Keep this in mind: it is a subtle point that should not be overlooked. On “We then establish a correspondence, etc.” This—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I161.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I160.lower.final","join_out":"I162.lower.01","readings":[{"locus":"لا ينبغي أن يمسك فيها","status":"The final verb is insecure; the English conveys the warning without treating its exact Arabic letters as settled."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I161.paratext.01","volume":1,"page":161,"physical_pdf_page":174,"layer":"paratext","attribution":"Printed gathering signature","arabic":"(١١ ـ تقريب أول)","english":"[Gathering signature:] 11 — Taqrīb, first part.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I161.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I161.ruled.01","volume":1,"page":161,"physical_pdf_page":174,"layer":"ruled","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله لزم تساوي الخ) وأقول تساوي الزائد والناقص إنما يستحيل في المتناهي وأما في غير المتناهي فلا لعدم التناهي وقد أوضحت ذلك في حواشي شرح العقائد فراجع اه منه","english":"On “They must be equal, etc.” I say: equality between a greater and a lesser collection is impossible only in the finite. In the infinite it is not, precisely because it is infinite. I have explained this in the glosses on Sharḥ al-ʿAqāʾid; consult them. End. “From him.”","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I161.jpg","stage":69,"editorial":"The printed first-person cross-reference is retained. It does not by itself establish the individual author in this mixed-note edition.","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I161.ruled.02","volume":1,"page":161,"physical_pdf_page":174,"layer":"ruled","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"وقد يقال أيضا وليس لك أن تقول لا مساواة ولا تفاوت لعدم التناهي وإنما هما عند التحديد لأنا نقول ذلك إنما هو في غير المتناهي بمعنى أن لا ينتهي في الوجود بالفعل مع عدم التناهي فإن أحد الأمرين لازم فيه قطعا لأن تحدده بالوجود حاصل منه اه منه","english":"It may also be said: you cannot reply that there is neither equality nor inequality because the collection is infinite, and that these apply only when something is bounded. We answer that this applies only to “infinite” in the sense of never coming to an end in actual existence. With [an actually existent] infinity, one of the two alternatives necessarily obtains, since determination through existence is present. End. “From him.”","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I161.jpg","stage":69,"readings":[{"locus":"بمعنى أن لا ينتهي في الوجود بالفعل مع عدم التناهي ... حاصل منه","status":"The contrast between potential unendingness and an actually existent infinity is compressed; one connective is insecure and the bracketed English is explanatory."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I161.upper.01","volume":1,"page":161,"physical_pdf_page":174,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"جملتان إحداهما من المعلول المحض والثانية من الذي فوقه (ثم نطبق بين هاتين الجملتين) من طرفهما المتناهي فالأول من إحداهما بإزاء الأول من الأخرى والثاني بإزاء الثاني وهلم (فإن وقع بإزاء كل جزء من) الجملة (التامة جزء من) الجملة (الناقصة لزم تساوي الكل) وهو التامة (والجزء) وهو","english":"—two series: one beginning with the sheer effect, and the second with the member above it. “We then establish a one-to-one correspondence between these two series,” starting from their finite ends: the first member of one is paired with the first of the other, the second with the second, and so on. “If every member of the complete series has opposite it a member of the deficient series, the whole”—the complete series—“and the part”—the deficient series—“must be equal,” which—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I161.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I160.upper.final","join_out":"I162.upper.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I162.lower.01","volume":1,"page":162,"physical_pdf_page":175,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"البرهان يسمى برهان التطبيق وعليه التعويل في كل ما يدعى تناهيه كما قالوا ثم الحق أن التطبيق الإجمالي العقلي كاف في جريانه خلافا لمن خالف في ذلك كما لا يخفى على من راجع المطولات","english":"—argument is called the proof by correspondence (burhān al-taṭbīq), and, they say, it is relied upon in every case where finitude is claimed. The truth is that an overall intellectual correspondence suffices to run the argument, contrary to those who disagree, as is clear to readers of the fuller works.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I162.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I161.lower.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I162.lower.02","volume":1,"page":162,"physical_pdf_page":175,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله وهو محال ضرورة الخ) اعترض بأنا نختار أنه يقع بإزاء كل من التامة جزء من الناقصة ولا نسلم لزوم التساوي فإن ذلك يجوز أن يكون لعدم التناهي لا للتساوي ولو سمى مجرد ذلك تساويا فلا نسلم استحالته فيما بين التامة والناقصة التي قدر شيء من جانبها المتناهي إنما استحيل فيما بين المتناهيين المتفاوتين يكون عدد أحدهما فوق عدد الآخر لا بين غير المتناهيين وذلك كما يقال مقدورات الله تعالى لاختصاصها بالممكنات أقل من معلوماته لشمولها الممتنعات أيضا والحاصل من تضعيف الواحد مرارا غير متناهية أقل من الحاصل من تضعيف الاثنين كذلك إلى غير ذلك مع لا تناهي السلسلتين وأجيب بدعوى الضرورة في ذلك كما أشار إليه رحمه الله وبأنا لا نسلم لا تناهي المقدورات ونحوها بالمعنى الذي نحن فيه بل بمعنى أنها لا تقف عند حد لا يكون فوقه مقدور أو معلوم أو مدد آخر وبهذا يجاب عن نقض أصل الدليل بأنه لو صح لزم أن تكون سلسلة الأعداد ونحوها متناهية لكن أقول في كون لا تناهي المعلومات بالمعنى اللايق تأمل فليتأمل والتفصيل موكول إلى المفصلات","english":"On “It is self-evidently impossible, etc.” The objection chooses the alternative in which every member of the complete series has a corresponding member in the deficient one, but denies that equality follows. The correspondence may result from infinity, not equality. Even if this alone is called equality, its impossibility is not conceded between an infinite complete series and one shortened at its finite end. Such equality is impossible only for two finite collections of unequal number, not for infinite ones. Thus, it is said that God’s objects of power, restricted to possibilities, are fewer than His objects of knowledge, which include impossibilities; and the result of doubling one without end is less than the result of doubling two without end, although both sequences are infinite. The reply appeals to the self-evidence of the impossibility, as he—may God have mercy on him—indicates. It also denies that the objects of power and similar cases are infinite in the relevant sense: they are infinite because they stop at no limit beyond which there could not be another object of power, knowledge or extension. This likewise answers the objection that, if the proof were sound, the sequence of numbers and the like would have to be finite. But I say: whether the infinity of the objects of knowledge has this [latter] meaning deserves consideration. Let it be considered. Fuller explanation belongs to the extensive works.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I162.jpg","stage":69,"readings":[{"locus":"الناقصة التي قدر شيء / أو مدد آخر / بالمعنى اللايق","status":"Several short expressions are insecure. The counterexamples and distinction between an actual infinite series and an unending increase are preserved without asserting a settled reading of those expressions."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I162.upper.01","volume":1,"page":162,"physical_pdf_page":175,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"الناقصة وهو محال ضرورة (وإلا) يكن بإزاء كل جزء من الزائدة جزء من الناقصة وجد في الزائدة جزء لا يوجد بإزائه من الناقصة شيء فعند هذا الجزء (انقطعت الناقصة فتناهت التامة) لأنها لا تزيد عليها إلا بواحد والزائد على المتناهي بالقدر المتناهي متناه بلا شبهة فلزم التناهي على تقدير اللاتناهي وهو محال (ولأنها) أي سلسلة العلل والمعلولات (لما اشتملت على معلول محض) لا يكون علة لشيء (لزم اشتمالها على علة محضة) لم تكن معلولة لشيء (تحقيقا للتكافؤ) بين المتضايفين فإن العلية والمعلولية متضايفتان ومن لوازمهما التكافؤ في الوجود بمعنى إذا وجد أحد المتضايفين وجد الآخر فلا بد أن يوجد","english":"—is self-evidently impossible. “Otherwise,” if some member of the greater series has no corresponding member in the lesser, the greater contains a member opposite which there is nothing in the lesser. At that member, “the lesser series ends, and the complete series is therefore finite,” since it exceeds the lesser by only one. A finite addition to the finite is unquestionably finite. Finitude would thus follow from the supposition of infinity, which is impossible. “And because that series”—of causes and effects—“contains a sheer effect,” which causes nothing, “it must contain a sheer cause,” caused by nothing, “to secure the correspondence” of correlatives. Being a cause and being an effect are correlatives; a requisite of correlation is correspondence in existence: if one relative exists, so does the other. There must therefore be—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I162.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I161.upper.01","join_out":"I163.upper.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I163.lower.01","volume":1,"page":163,"physical_pdf_page":176,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله مع اشتمالها على معلول الخ) قد يقال إن المكافئ لمعلولية المعلول المحض هو علية الذي فوقه بلا واسطة وإن كان معلولا أيضا لا علية العلة المحضة ويجاب بأنه نعم كذلك لكن المراد أنه لا بد أن يكون بإزاء كل معلولية علية وهذا يقتضي ثبوت العلة المحضة كما قالوا (قوله ولأنا نعزل المعلول المحض الخ) هذا هو الاستدلال السابق كما في شرح المقاصد إلا أنه يغايره بالعبارة والاعتبار","english":"On “While containing a sheer effect, etc.” It may be objected that the correlate of the sheer effect’s being an effect is the causality of the member immediately above it, even if that is itself an effect—not the causality of a sheer cause. The reply admits this, but says that the point is that every effect-relation must have a corresponding cause-relation, which, they maintain, requires a sheer cause. On “And because we set aside the sheer effect, etc.” This is the preceding argument, as in Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid, differing only in wording and consideration.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I163.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false}]);